[0.009023, "o", "\u001b[?1049h\u001b[22;0;0t\u001b[50;1H\u001b[?1h\u001b=\r"]
[0.022821, "o", "\u001b[4mTMUX\u001b[24m(1)                                                                                  General Commands Manual                                                                                \u001b[4mTMUX\u001b[24m(1)\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mNAME\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       tmux — terminal multiplexer\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mSYNOPSIS\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-2CDlNuVv\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mshell-command\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfile\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-L \u001b[4m\u001b[22msocket-name\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-S \u001b[4m\u001b[22msocket-path\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-T \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfeatures\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m [\u001b[4mflags\u001b[24m]]\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mDESCRIPTION\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtmux  \u001b[22mis  a terminal multiplexer: it enables a number of terminals to be created, accessed, and controlled from a single screen.  \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mmay be detached from a screen and continue running in the\u001b[m\r\r\n       background, then later reattached.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       When \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mis started, it creates a new \u001b[4msession\u001b[24m with a single \u001b[4mwindow\u001b[24m and displays it on screen.  A status line at the bottom of the screen s"]
[0.022938, "o", "hows information on the current session and  is  used\u001b[m\r\r\n       to enter interactive commands.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       A  session  is  a  single collection of \u001b[4mpseudo\u001b[24m \u001b[4mterminals\u001b[24m under the management of \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m.  Each session has one or more windows linked to it.  A window occupies the entire screen and may be split\u001b[m\r\r\n       into rectangular panes, each of which is a separate pseudo terminal (the \u001b[4mpty\u001b[24m(4) manual page documents the technical details of pseudo terminals).  Any number of \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22minstances may  connect  to\u001b[m\r\r\n       the same session, and any number of windows may be present in the same session.  Once all sessions are killed, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mexits.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Each session is persistent and will survive accidental disconnection (such as \u001b[4mssh\u001b[24m(1) connection timeout) or intentional detaching (with the ‘C-b d’ key strokes).  \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mmay be reattached using:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m$ tmux attach\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       In  \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m, a session is displayed on screen by a \u001b[4mclient\u001b[24m and all sessions are managed by a single \u001b[4mserver\u001b[24m.  The server and each client are separate processes which communicate through a socket in\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4m/tmp\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The options are as follows:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-2            \u001b[22mForce \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mto assume the terminal supports 256 colours.  This is equivalent to \u001b[1m-T \u001b[4m\u001b[22m256\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-C            \u001b[22mStart in control mode (see the “CONTROL MODE” section).  Given twice (\u001b[1m-CC\u001b[22m) disables echo.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mshell-command\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     Execute \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m using the default shell.  If necessary, the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver will be started to retrieve the \u001b[1mdefault-shell \u001b[22moption.  This option is for compatibility  with  \u001b[4msh\u001b[24m(1)\u001b[m\r\r\n                     when \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mis used as a login shell.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-D            \u001b[22mDo not start the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver as a daemon.  This also turns the \u001b[1mexit-empty \u001b[22moption off.  With \u001b[1m-D\u001b[22m, \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m may not be specified.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfile\u001b[24m       Specify  an  alternative  configuration  file.   By default, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mloads the system configuration file from \u001b[4m/etc/tmux.conf\u001b[24m, if present, then looks for a user configuration file at\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[4m~/.tmux.conf\u001b[24m or \u001b[4m$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux/tmux.conf\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     The configuration file is a set of \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mcommands which are executed in sequence when the server is first started.  \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mloads configuration files once when the server process has\u001b[m\r\r\n                     started.  The \u001b[1msource-file \u001b[22mcommand may be used to load a file later.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mshows any error messages from commands in configuration files in the first session created, and continues to process the rest of the configuration file.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-L \u001b[4m\u001b[22msocket-name\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[0.024757, "o", "                     \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mstores the server socket in a directory under TMUX_TMPDIR or \u001b[4m/tmp\u001b[24m if it is unset.  The default socket is named \u001b[4mdefault\u001b[24m.  This option allows a different  socket  name  to  be\u001b[m\r\r\n                     specified,  allowing  several independent \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mservers to be run.  Unlike \u001b[1m-S \u001b[22ma full path is not necessary: the sockets are all created in a directory \u001b[4mtmux-UID\u001b[24m under the directory\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.016088, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[1.016568, "o", "                     given by TMUX_TMPDIR or in \u001b[4m/tmp\u001b[24m.  The \u001b[4mtmux-UID\u001b[24m directory is created by \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mand must not be world readable, writable or executable.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     If the socket is accidentally removed, the SIGUSR1 signal may be sent to the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver process to recreate it (note that this will fail if any parent directories are missing).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-l            \u001b[22mBehave as a login shell.  This flag currently has no effect and is for compatibility with other shells when using tmux as a login shell.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-N            \u001b[22mDo not start the server even if the command would normally do so (for example \u001b[1mnew-session \u001b[22mor \u001b[1mstart-server\u001b[22m).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-S \u001b[4m\u001b[22msocket-path\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     Specify a full alternative path to the server socket.  If \u001b[1m-S \u001b[22mis specified, the default socket directory is not used and any \u001b[1m-L \u001b[22mflag is ignored.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-T \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfeatures\u001b[24m   Set terminal features for the client.  This is a comma-separated list of features.  See the \u001b[1mterminal-features \u001b[22moption.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-u            \u001b[22mWrite UTF-8 output to the terminal even if the first environment variable of LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE, or LANG that is set does not contain \"UTF-8\" or \"UTF8\".\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-V            \u001b[22mReport the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mversion.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m-v            \u001b[22mRequest verbose logging.  Log messages will be saved into \u001b[4mtmux-client-PID.log\u001b[24m and \u001b[4mtmux-server-PID.log\u001b[24m files in the current directory, where \u001b[4mPID\u001b[24m is the PID of the server or client\u001b[m\r\r\n                     process.  If \u001b[1m-v \u001b[22mis specified twice, an additional \u001b[4mtmux-out-PID.log\u001b[24m file is generated with a copy of everything \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwrites to the terminal.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     The SIGUSR2 signal may be sent to the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver process to toggle logging between on (as if \u001b[1m-v \u001b[22mwas given) and off.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m [\u001b[4mflags\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     This specifies one of a set of commands used to control \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m, as described in the following sections.  If no commands are specified, the \u001b[1mnew-session \u001b[22mcommand is assumed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mDEFAULT KEY BINDINGS\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mmay be controlled from an attached client by using a key combination of a prefix key, ‘C-b’ (Ctrl-b) by default, followed by a command key.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The default command key bindings are:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             C-b         Send the prefix key (C-b) through to the application.\u001b[m\r\r\n             C-o         Rotate the panes in the current window forwards.\u001b[m\r\r\n             C-z         Suspend the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mclient.\u001b[m\r\r\n             !           Break the current pane out of the window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             \"           Split the current pane into two, top and bottom.\u001b[m\r\r\n             #           List all paste buffers.\u001b[m\r\r\n             $           Rename the current session.\u001b[m\r\r\n             %           Split the current pane into two, left and right.\u001b[m\r\r\n "]
[1.016635, "o", "            &           Kill the current window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             '           Prompt for a window index to select.\u001b[m\r\r\n             (           Switch the attached client to the previous session.\u001b[m\r\r\n             )           Switch the attached client to the next session.\u001b[m\r\r\n             ,           Rename the current window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             -           Delete the most recently copied buffer of text.\u001b[m\r\r\n             .           Prompt for an index to move the current window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             0 to 9      Select windows 0 to 9.\u001b[m\r\r\n             :           Enter the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mcommand prompt.\u001b[m\r\r\n             ;           Move to the previously active pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n             =           Choose which buffer to paste interactively from a list.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 50 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.097346, "o", "\r\u001b[K             ?           List all key bindings.\u001b[m\r\r\n             D           Choose a client to detach.\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[1.097799, "o", "             L           Switch the attached client back to the last session.\u001b[m\r\r\n             [           Enter copy mode to copy text or view the history.\u001b[m\r\r\n             ]           Paste the most recently copied buffer of text.\u001b[m\r\r\n             c           Create a new window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             d           Detach the current client.\u001b[m\r\r\n             f           Prompt to search for text in open windows.\u001b[m\r\r\n             i           Display some information about the current window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             l           Move to the previously selected window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             m           Mark the current pane (see \u001b[1mselect-pane -m\u001b[22m).\u001b[m\r\r\n             M           Clear the marked pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n             n           Change to the next window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             o           Select the next pane in the current window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             p           Change to the previous window.\u001b[m\r\r\n             q           Briefly display pane indexes.\u001b[m\r\r\n             r           Force redraw of the attached client.\u001b[m\r\r\n             s           Select a new session for the attached client interactively.\u001b[m\r\r\n             t           Show the time.\u001b[m\r\r\n             w           Choose the current window interactively.\u001b[m\r\r\n             x           Kill the current pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n             z           Toggle zoom state of the current pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n             {           Swap the current pane with the previous pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n             }           Swap the current pane with the next pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n             ~           Show previous messages from \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m, if any.\u001b[m\r\r\n             Page Up     Enter copy mode and scroll one page up.\u001b[m\r\r\n             Up, Down\u001b[m\r\r\n             Left, Right\u001b[m\r\r\n                         Change to the pane above, below, to the left, or to the right of the current pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n             M-1 to M-5  Arrange panes in one of the seven preset layouts: even-horizontal, even-vertical, main-horizontal, main-horizontal-mirrored, main-vertical, main-vertical, or tiled.\u001b[m\r\r\n             Space       Arrange the current window in the next preset layout.\u001b[m\r\r\n             M-n         Move to the next window with a bell or activity marker.\u001b[m\r\r\n             M-o         Rotate the panes in the current window backwards.\u001b[m\r\r\n             M-p         Move to the previous window with a bell or activity marker.\u001b[m\r\r\n             C-Up, C-Down\u001b[m\r\r\n             C-Left, C-Right\u001b[m\r\r\n                         Resize the current pane in steps of one cell.\u001b[m\r\r\n             M-Up, M-Down\u001b[m\r\r\n             M-Left, M-Right\u001b[m\r\r\n                         Resize the current pane in steps of five cells.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Key bindings may be changed with the \u001b[1mbind-key \u001b[22mand \u001b[1munbind-key \u001b[22mcommands.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mCOMMAND PARSING AND EXECUTION\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22msupports a large number of commands which can be used to control its behaviour.  Each command is named and can accept zero or more flags and arguments.  They may be bound to  a  key  with\u001b[m\r\r\n       the  \u001b[1mbind-key  \u001b[22mcommand  or  run  from  the  shell  prompt, a shell script, a configuration file or the command prompt.  For example, the same \u001b[1mset-option \u001b[22mcommand run from the shell prompt, from\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4m~/.tmux.conf\u001b[24m and bound to a key may look like:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux set-option -g status-style bg=cyan\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 99 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.178745, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[1.179063, "o", "\u001b[m\r\r\n             set-option -g status-style bg=cyan\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             bind-key C set-option -g status-style bg=cyan\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Here, the command name is ‘set-option’, ‘\u001b[1m-g\u001b[22m’ is a flag and ‘status-style’ and ‘bg=cyan’ are arguments.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mdistinguishes between command parsing and execution.  In order to execute a command, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mneeds it to be split up into its name and arguments.  This is command parsing.  If a  command  is\u001b[m\r\r\n       run from the shell, the shell parses it; from inside \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mor from a configuration file, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mdoes.  Examples of when \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mparses commands are:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22min a configuration file;\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mtyped at the command prompt (see \u001b[1mcommand-prompt\u001b[22m);\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mgiven to \u001b[1mbind-key\u001b[22m;\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mpassed as arguments to \u001b[1mif-shell \u001b[22mor \u001b[1mconfirm-before\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       To  execute commands, each client has a ‘command queue’.  A global command queue not attached to any client is used on startup for configuration files like \u001b[4m~/.tmux.conf\u001b[24m.  Parsed commands added\u001b[m\r\r\n       to the queue are executed in order.  Some commands, like \u001b[1mif-shell \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mconfirm-before\u001b[22m, parse their argument to create a new command which is inserted immediately after themselves.   This  means\u001b[m\r\r\n       that arguments can be parsed twice or more - once when the parent command (such as \u001b[1mif-shell\u001b[22m) is parsed and again when it parses and executes its command.  Commands like \u001b[1mif-shell\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mrun-shell \u001b[22mand\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdisplay-panes  \u001b[22mstop execution of subsequent commands on the queue until something happens - \u001b[1mif-shell \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mrun-shell \u001b[22muntil a shell command finishes and \u001b[1mdisplay-panes \u001b[22muntil a key is pressed.  For\u001b[m\r\r\n       example, the following commands:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             new-session; new-window\u001b[m\r\r\n             if-shell \"true\" \"split-window\"\u001b[m\r\r\n             kill-session\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Will execute \u001b[1mnew-session\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mnew-window\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mif-shell\u001b[22m, the shell command \u001b[4mtrue\u001b[24m(1), \u001b[1msplit-window \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mkill-session \u001b[22min that order.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The “COMMANDS” section lists the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mcommands and their arguments.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mPARSING SYNTAX\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       This section describes the syntax of commands parsed by \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m, for example in a configuration file or at the command prompt.  Note that when commands are entered into the shell, they are parsed\u001b[m\r\r\n       by the shell - see for example \u001b[4mksh\u001b[24m(1) or \u001b[4mcsh\u001b[24m(1).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Each command is terminated by a newline or a semicolon (;).  Commands separated by semicolons together form a ‘command sequence’ - if a command in the sequence encounters an error,  no  subse‐\u001b[m\r\r\n       quent commands are executed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       It is recommended that a semicolon used as a command separator should be written as an individual token, for example from \u001b[4msh\u001b[24m(1):\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux neww \\; splitw\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Or:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux neww ';' splitw\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Or from the tmux command prompt:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 148 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.268429, "o", "\r\u001b[K             neww ; splitw\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       However, a trailing semicolon is also interpreted as a command separator, for example in these \u001b[4msh\u001b[24m(1) commands:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux neww\\; splitw\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Or:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux 'neww;' splitw\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       As in these examples, when running tmux from the shell extra care must be taken to properly quote semicolons:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             1.   Semicolons that should be interpreted as a command separator should be escaped according to the shell conventions.  For \u001b[4msh\u001b[24m(1) this typically means quoted (such as ‘neww ';' splitw’)\u001b[m\r\r\n                  or escaped (such as ‘neww \\\\\\\\; splitw’).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             2.   Individual semicolons or trailing semicolons that should be interpreted as arguments should be escaped twice: once according to the shell conventions and a second time for \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m; for\u001b[m\r\r\n                  example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                        $ tmux neww 'foo\\\\;' bar\u001b[m\r\r\n                        $ tmux neww foo\\\\\\\\; "]
[1.268943, "o", "bar\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             3.   Semicolons that are not individual tokens or trailing another token should only be escaped once according to shell conventions; for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                        $ tmux neww 'foo-;-bar'\u001b[m\r\r\n                        $ tmux neww foo-\\\\;-bar\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Comments are marked by the unquoted # character - any remaining text after a comment is ignored until the end of the line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       If  the  last character of a line is \\, the line is joined with the following line (the \\ and the newline are completely removed).  This is called line continuation and applies both inside and\u001b[m\r\r\n       outside quoted strings and in comments, but not inside braces.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Command arguments may be specified as strings surrounded by single (') quotes, double quotes (\") or braces ({}).  This is required when the argument contains any special character.  Single and\u001b[m\r\r\n       double quoted strings cannot span multiple lines except with line continuation.  Braces can span multiple lines.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[1.269223, "o", "       Outside of quotes and inside double quotes, these replacements are performed:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mEnvironment variables preceded by $ are replaced with their value from the global environment (see the “GLOBAL AND SESSION ENVIRONMENT” section).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mA leading ~ or ~user is expanded to the home directory of the current or specified user.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22m\\uXXXX or \\uXXXXXXXX is replaced by the Unicode codepoint corresponding to the given four or eight digit hexadecimal number.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mWhen preceded (escaped) by a \\, the following characters are replaced: \\e by the escape character; \\r by a carriage return; \\n by a newline; and \\t by a tab.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22m\\ooo is replaced by a character of the octal value ooo.  Three octal digits are required, for example \\001.  The largest valid character is \\377.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mAny other characters preceded by \\ are replaced by themselves (that is, the \\ is removed) and are not treated as having any special meaning - so for example \\; will not mark  a  com‐\u001b[m\r\r\n                 mand sequence and \\$ will not expand an environment variable.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 197 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.353936, "o", "\r\u001b[K       Braces  are parsed as a configuration file (so conditions such as ‘%if’ are processed) and then converted into a string.  They are designed to avoid the need for additional escaping when pass‐\u001b[m\r\r\n       ing a group of \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mcommands as an argument (for example to \u001b[1mif-shell\u001b[22m).  These two examples produce an identical command - note that no escaping is needed when using {}:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             if-shell true {\u001b[m\r\r\n                 display -p 'brace-dollar-foo: }$foo'\u001b[m\r\r\n             }\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             if-shell true \"display -p 'brace-dollar-foo: }\\$foo'\"\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Braces may be enclosed inside braces, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             bind x if-shell \"true\" {\u001b[m\r\r\n                 if-shell \"true\" {\u001b[m\r\r\n                     display \"true!\"\u001b[m\r\r\n                 }\u001b[m\r\r\n             }\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Environment variables may be set by using the syntax ‘name=value’, for example ‘HOME=/home/user’.  Variables set during parsing are added to the global environment.  A hidden variable  "]
[1.354764, "o", "may  be\u001b[m\r\r\n       set with ‘%hidden’, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             %hidden MYVAR=42\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Hidden variables are not passed to the environment of processes created by tmux.  See the “GLOBAL AND SESSION ENVIRONMENT” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Commands  may be parsed conditionally by surrounding them with ‘%if’, ‘%elif’, ‘%else’ and ‘%endif’.  The argument to ‘%if’ and ‘%elif’ is expanded as a format (see “FORMATS”) and if it evalu‐\u001b[m\r\r\n       ates to false (zero or empty), subsequent text is ignored until the closing ‘%elif’, ‘%else’ or ‘%endif’.  For example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             %if \"#{==:#{host},myhost}\"\u001b[m\r\r\n             set -g status-style bg=red\u001b[m\r\r\n             %elif \"#{==:#{host},myotherhost}\"\u001b[m\r\r\n             set -g status-style bg=green\u001b[m\r\r\n             %else\u001b[m\r\r\n             set -g status-style bg=blue\u001b[m\r\r\n             %endif\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Will change the status line to red if running on ‘myhost’, green if running on ‘myotherhost’, or blue if running on another host.  Conditionals may be given on one line, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             %if #{==:#{host},myhost} set -g status-style bg=red %endif\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mCOMMANDS\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       This section describes the commands supported by \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m.  Most commands accept the optional \u001b[1m-t  \u001b[22m(and  sometimes  \u001b[1m-s\u001b[22m)  argument  with  one  of  \u001b[4mtarget-client\u001b[24m,  \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m,  \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m,  or\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m.  These specify the client, session, window or pane which a command should affect.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4mtarget-client\u001b[24m should be the name of the client, typically the \u001b[4mpty\u001b[24m(4) file to which the client is connected, for example either of \u001b[4m/dev/ttyp1\u001b[24m or \u001b[4mttyp1\u001b[24m for the client attached to \u001b[4m/dev/ttyp1\u001b[24m.  If\u001b[m\r\r\n       no client is specified, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mattempts to work out the client currently in use; if that fails, an error is reported.  Clients may be listed with the \u001b[1mlist-clients \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4mtarg"]
[1.355001, "o", "et-session\u001b[24m is tried as, in order:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             1.   A session ID prefixed with a $.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 246 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.438814, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[1.43927, "o", "\u001b[m\r\r\n             2.   An exact name of a session (as listed by the \u001b[1mlist-sessions \u001b[22mcommand).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             3.   The start of a session name, for example ‘mysess’ would match a session named ‘mysession’.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             4.   An \u001b[4mfnmatch\u001b[24m(3) pattern which is matched against the session name.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       If the session name is prefixed with an ‘=’, only an exact match is accepted (so ‘=mysess’ will only match exactly ‘mysess’, not ‘mysession’).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       If  a single session is found, it is used as the target session; multiple matches produce an error.  If a session is omitted, the current session is used if available; if no current session is\u001b[m\r\r\n       available, the most recently used is chosen.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m (or \u001b[4msrc-window\u001b[24m or \u001b[4mdst-window\u001b[24m) specifies a window in the form \u001b[4msession\u001b[24m:\u001b[4mwindow\u001b[24m.  \u001b[4msession\u001b[24m follows the same rules as for \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m, and \u001b[4mwindow\u001b[24m is looked for in order as:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             1.   A special token, listed below.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             2.   A window index, for example ‘mysession:1’ is window 1 in session ‘mysession’.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             3.   A window ID, such as @1.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             4.   An exact window name, such as ‘mysession:mywindow’.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             5.   The start of a window name, such as ‘mysession:mywin’.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             6.   As an \u001b[4mfnmatch\u001b[24m(3) pattern matched against the window name.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Like sessions, a ‘=’ prefix will do an exact match only.  An empty window name specifies the next unused index if appropriate (for example the \u001b[1mnew-window \u001b[22mand  \u001b[1mlink-window  \u001b[22mcommands)  otherwise\u001b[m\r\r\n       the current window in \u001b[4msession\u001b[24m is chosen.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The following special tokens are available to indicate particular windows.  Each has a single-character alternative form.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mToken              Meaning\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{start}       \u001b[22m^    The lowest-numbered window\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{end}         \u001b[22m$    The highest-numbered window\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{last}        \u001b[22m!    The last (previously current) window\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{next}        \u001b[22m+    The next window by number\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{previous}    \u001b[22m-    The previous window by number\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m  (or  \u001b[4msrc-pane\u001b[24m  or  \u001b[4mdst-pane\u001b[24m) may be a pane ID or takes a similar form to \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m but with the optional addition of a period followed by a pane index or pane ID, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n       ‘mysession:mywindow.1’.  If the pane index is omitted, the currently active pane in the specified window is used.  The following special tokens are available for the pane index:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mToken                  Meaning\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{last}            \u001b[22m!    The last (previously active) pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{next}            \u001b[22m+    The next pane by number\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{previous}        \u001b[22m-    The previous pane by number\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{top}                  \u001b[22mThe top pane\u001b"]
[1.439925, "o", "[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{bottom}               \u001b[22mThe bottom pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{left}                 \u001b[22mThe leftmost pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{right}                \u001b[22mThe rightmost pane\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 295 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.52157, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[1.521635, "o", "       \u001b[1m{top-left}             \u001b[22mThe top-left pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{top-right}            \u001b[22mThe top-right pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{bottom-left}          \u001b[22mThe bottom-left pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{bottom-right}         \u001b[22mThe bottom-right pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{up-of}                \u001b[22mThe pane above the active pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{down-of}              \u001b[22mThe pane below the active pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{left-of}              \u001b[22mThe pane to the left of the active pane\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1m{right-of}             \u001b[22mThe pane to the right of the active pane\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The tokens ‘+’ and ‘-’ may be followed by an offset, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             select-window -t:+2\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       In addition, \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m or \u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m may consist entirely of the token ‘{mouse}’ (alternative form ‘=’) to specify the session, window or pane where the most recent  mouse\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[1.521902, "o", "       event occurred (see the “MOUSE SUPPORT” section) or ‘{marked}’ (alternative form ‘~’) to specify the marked pane (see \u001b[1mselect-pane -m\u001b[22m).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Sessions,  window  and panes are each numbered with a unique ID; session IDs are prefixed with a ‘$’, windows with a ‘@’, and panes with a ‘%’.  These are unique and are unchanged for the life\u001b[m\r\r\n       of the session, window or pane in the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver.  The pane ID is passed to the child process of the pane in the TMUX_PANE environment variable.  IDs may be displayed using the  ‘session_id’,\u001b[m\r\r\n       ‘window_id’, or ‘pane_id’ formats (see the “FORMATS” section) and the \u001b[1mdisplay-message\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mlist-sessions\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mlist-windows \u001b[22mor \u001b[1mlist-panes \u001b[22mcommands.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m arguments are \u001b[4msh\u001b[24m(1) commands.  This may be a single argument passed to the shell, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             new-window 'vi ~/.tmux.conf'\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Will run:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             /bin/sh -c 'vi ~/.tmux.conf'\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Additionally, the \u001b[1mnew-window\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mnew-session\u001b[22m, \u001b[1msplit-window\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mrespawn-window \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mrespawn-pane \u001b[22mcommands allow \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m to be given as multiple arguments and executed directly (without ‘sh -c’).\u001b[m\r\r\n       This can avoid issues with shell quoting.  For example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux new-window vi ~/.tmux.conf\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Will run \u001b[4mvi\u001b[24m(1) directly without invoking the shell.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m [\u001b[4margument\u001b[24m \u001b[4m...\u001b[24m] refers to a \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mcommand, either passed with the command and arguments separately, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             bind-key F1 set-option status off\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Or passed as a single string argument in \u001b[4m.tmux.conf\u001b[24m, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             bind-key F1 { set-option status off }\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Example \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mcommands include:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             refresh-client -t/dev/ttyp2\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             rename-session -tfirst newname\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 344 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.604855, "o", "\r\u001b[K             set-option -wt:0 monitor-activity on\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             new-window ; split-window -d\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             bind-key R source-file ~/.tmux.conf \\; \\\u001b[m\r\r\n                     display-message \"source-file done\"\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Or from \u001b[4msh\u001b[24m(1):\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux kill-window -t :1\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux new-window \\; split-window -d\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux new-session -d 'vi ~/.tmux.conf' \\; split-window -d \\; attach\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mCLIENTS AND SESSIONS\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       The  \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver manages clients, sessions, windows and panes.  Clients are attached to sessions to interact with them, either when they are created with the \u001b[1mnew-session \u001b[22mcommand, or later with\u001b[m\r\r\n       the \u001b[1mattach-session \u001b[22mcommand.  Each session has one or more windows \u001b[4mlinked\u001b[24m into it.  Windows may be linked to multiple sessions and are made up of one or more panes, each  of  which  contains  a\u001b[m\r\r\n       pseudo terminal.  Commands for creating, linking and otherwise manipulating windows are"]
[1.605363, "o", " covered in the “WINDOWS AND PANES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The following commands are available to manage clients and sessions:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mattach-session \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-dErx\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mworking-directory\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mflags\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mattach\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               If run from outside \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m, attach to \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m in the current terminal.  \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m must already exist - to create a new session, see the \u001b[1mnew-session \u001b[22mcommand (with \u001b[1m-A \u001b[22mto create\u001b[m\r\r\n               or attach).  If used from inside, switch the currently attached session to \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m.  If \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mis specified, any other clients attached to the session are detached.  If \u001b[1m-x \u001b[22mis given,\u001b[m\r\r\n               send SIGHUP to the parent process of the client as well as detaching the client, typically causing it to exit.  \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22msets a comma-separated list of client flags.  The flags are:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               active-pane\u001b[m\r\r\n                       the client has an independent active pane\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               ignore-size\u001b[m\r\r\n                       the client does not affect the size of other clients\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               no-output\u001b[m\r\r\n                       the client does not receive pane output in control mode\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               pause-after=seconds\u001b[m\r\r\n                       output is paused once the pane is \u001b[4mseconds\u001b[24m behind in control mode\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               read-only\u001b[m\r\r\n                       the client is read-only\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               wait-exit\u001b[m\r\r\n                       wait for an empty line input before exiting in control mode\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               A  leading  ‘!’  turns  a flag off if the client is already attached.  \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mis an alias for \u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mread-only,ignore-size\u001b[24m.  When a client is read-only, only keys bound to the \u001b[1mdetach-client \u001b[22mor\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mswitch-client \u001b[22mcommands have any effect.  A client with the \u001b[4mactive-pane\u001b[24m flag allows the active pane to be selected independently of the window's active pane used by clients without  the\u001b[m\r\r\n               flag.  This only affects the cursor position and commands issued from the client; other features such as hooks and styles continue to use the window's active pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 393 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.688692, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[1.689048, "o", "\u001b[m\r\r\n               If no server is started, \u001b[1mattach-session \u001b[22mwill attempt to start it; this will fail unless sessions are created in the configuration file.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m rules for \u001b[1mattach-session \u001b[22mare slightly adjusted: if \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mneeds to select the most recently used session, it will prefer the most recently used \u001b[4munattached\u001b[24m session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-c \u001b[22mwill set the session working directory (used for new windows) to \u001b[4mworking-directory\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1m-E \u001b[22mis used, the \u001b[1mupdate-environment \u001b[22moption will not be applied.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdetach-client \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-aP\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-E \u001b[4m\u001b[22mshell-command\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-client\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mdetach\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Detach  the  current  client  if bound to a key, the client specified with \u001b[1m-t\u001b[22m, or all clients currently attached to the session specified by \u001b[1m-s\u001b[22m.  The \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22moption kills all but the client\u001b[m\r\r\n               given with \u001b[1m-t\u001b[22m.  If \u001b[1m-P \u001b[22mis given, send SIGHUP to the parent process of the client, typically causing it to exit.  With \u001b[1m-E\u001b[22m, run \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m to replace the client.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mhas-session \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mhas\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Report an error and exit with 1 if the specified session does not exist.  If it does exist, exit with 0.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mkill-server\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Kill the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver and clients and destroy all sessions.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mkill-session \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-aC\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Destroy the given session, closing any windows linked to it and no other sessions, and detaching all clients attached to it.  If \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mis given, all sessions  but  the  specified  one  is\u001b[m\r\r\n               killed.  The \u001b[1m-C \u001b[22mflag clears alerts (bell, activity, or silenc"]
[1.689868, "o", "e) in all windows linked to the session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlist-clients \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfilter\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlsc\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               List  all  clients  attached to the server.  \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mspecifies the format of each line and \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22ma filter.  Only clients for which the filter is true are shown.  See the “FORMATS” section.  If\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m is specified, list only clients connected to that session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlist-commands \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlscm\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               List the syntax of \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m or - if omitted - of all commands supported by \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlist-sessions \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfilter\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mls\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               List all sessions managed by the server.  \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mspecifies the format of each line and \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22ma filter.  Only sessions for which the filter is true are shown.  See the “FORMATS” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlock-client \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-client\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlockc\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Lock \u001b[4mtarget-client\u001b[24m, see the \u001b[1mlock-server \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlock-session \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlocks\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Lock all clients attached to \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnew-session \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-AdDEPX\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstart-directory\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-e \u001b[4m\u001b[22menvironment\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mflags\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-n \u001b[4m\u001b[22mwindow-name\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22msession-name\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mgroup-name\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-x \u001b[4m\u001b[22mwidth\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-y \u001b[4m\u001b[22mheight\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mnew\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Create a new session with name \u001b[4msession-name\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 442 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.77126, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n               The new session is attached to the current terminal unless \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mis given.  \u001b[4mwindow-name\u001b[24m and \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m are the name of and shell command to execute in the initial window.  With \u001b[1m-d\u001b[22m, the\u001b[m\r\r\n               initial size comes from the global \u001b[1mdefault-size \u001b[22moption; \u001b[1m-x \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-y \u001b[22mcan be used to specify a different size.  ‘-’ uses the size of the current client if any.  If \u001b[1m-x \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-y \u001b[22mis  given,  the\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mdefault-size \u001b[22moption is set for the session.  \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22msets a comma-separated list of client flags (see \u001b[1mattach-session\u001b[22m).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If run from a terminal, any \u001b[4mtermios\u001b[24m(4) special characters are saved and used for new windows in the new session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The \u001b[1m-A \u001b[22mflag makes \u001b[1mnew-session \u001b[22mbehave like \u001b[1mattach-session \u001b[22mif \u001b[4msession-name\u001b[24m already exists; if \u001b[1m-A \u001b[22mis given, \u001b[1m-D \u001b[22mbehaves like \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mto \u001b[1mattach-sessi"]
[1.771325, "o", "on\u001b[22m, and \u001b[1m-X \u001b[22mbehaves like \u001b[1m-x \u001b[22mto \u001b[1mattach-session\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1m-t \u001b[22mis given, it specifies a \u001b[1msession group\u001b[22m.  Sessions in the same group share the same set of windows - new windows are linked to all sessions in the group and any windows closed re‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               moved  from  all  sessions.   The  current  and  previous  window and any session options remain independent and any session in a group may be killed without affecting the others.  The\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[4mgroup-name\u001b[24m argument may be:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               1.      the name of an existing group, in which case the new session is added to that group;\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               2.      the name of an existing session - the new session is added to the same group as that session, creating a new group if necessary;\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               3.      the name for a new group containing only the new session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-n \u001b[22mand \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m are invalid if \u001b[1m-t \u001b[22mis used.\u001b["]
[1.771556, "o", "m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The \u001b[1m-P \u001b[22moption prints information about the new session after it has been created.  By default, it uses the format ‘#{session_name}:’ but a different format may be specified with \u001b[1m-F\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1m-E \u001b[22mis used, the \u001b[1mupdate-environment \u001b[22moption will not be applied.  \u001b[1m-e \u001b[22mtakes the form ‘VARIABLE=value’ and sets an environment variable for the newly created session; it may  be  speci‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               fied multiple times.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrefresh-client \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-cDLRSU\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-A \u001b[4m\u001b[22mpane:state\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-B \u001b[4m\u001b[22mname:what:format\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-C \u001b[4m\u001b[22msize\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mflags\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-l \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m]] [\u001b[1m-r \u001b[4m\u001b[22mpane:report\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-client\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mrefresh\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Refresh the current client if bound to a key, or a single client if one is given with \u001b[1m-t\u001b[22m.  If \u001b[1m-S \u001b[22mis specified, only update the client's status line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The  \u001b[1m-U\u001b[22m,  \u001b[1m-D\u001b[22m,  \u001b[1m-L -R\u001b[22m, and \u001b[1m-c \u001b[22mflags allow the visible portion of a window which is larger than the client to be changed.  \u001b[1m-U \u001b[22mmoves the visible part up by \u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m rows and \u001b[1m-D \u001b[22mdown, \u001b[1m-L\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               left by \u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m columns and \u001b[1m-R \u001b[22mright.  \u001b[1m-c \u001b[22mreturns to tracking the cursor automatically.  If \u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m is omitted, 1 is used.  Note that the visible position is a  property  of  the\u001b[m\r\r\n               client not of the window, changing the current window in the attached session will reset it.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-C  \u001b[22msets  the width and height of a control mode client or of a window for a control mode client, \u001b[4msize\u001b[24m must be one of ‘widthxheight’ or ‘window ID:widthxheight’, for example ‘80x24’ or\u001b[m\r\r\n               ‘@0:80x24’.  \u001b[1m-A \u001b[22mallows a control mode client to trigger actions on a pane.  The argument is a pane ID (with leading ‘%’), a colon, then one of ‘on’, ‘off’, ‘continue’ or  ‘pause’.   If\u001b[m\r\r\n               ‘off’,  \u001b[1mtmux  \u001b[22mwill not send output from the pane to the client and if all clients have turned the pane off, will stop reading from the pane.  If ‘continue’, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill return to sending\u001b[m\r\r\n               output to the pane if it was paused (manually or with the \u001b[4mpause-after\u001b[24m flag).  If ‘pause’, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill pause the pane.  \u001b[1m-A \u001b[22mmay be given multiple times for different panes.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-B \u001b[22msets a subscription to a format for a control mode client.  The argument is split into three items by colons: \u001b[4mname\u001b[24m is a name for the subscription; \u001b[4mwhat\u001b[24m is a type  of  item  to  sub‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               scribe  to;  \u001b[4mformat\u001b[24m  is  the format.  After a subscription is added, changes to the format are reported with the \u001b[1m%subscription-changed \u001b[22mnotification, at most once a second.  If only the\u001b[m\r\r\n   "]
[1.771861, "o", "            name is given, the subscription is removed.  \u001b[4mwhat\u001b[24m may be empty to check the format only for the attached session, or one of: a pane ID such as ‘%0’; ‘%*’ for all panes in the  attached\u001b[m\r\r\n               session; a window ID such as ‘@0’; or ‘@*’ for all windows in the attached session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-f  \u001b[22msets a comma-separated list of client flags, see \u001b[1mattach-session\u001b[22m.  \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mallows a control mode client to provide information about a pane via a report (such as the response to OSC 10).\u001b[m\r\r\n               The argument is a pane ID (with a leading ‘%’), a colon, then a report escape sequence.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-l \u001b[22mrequests the clipboard from the client using the \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1) escape sequence.  If \u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m is given, the clipboard is sent (in encoded form), otherwise it is stored in a  new  paste\u001b[m\r\r\n               buffer.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 491 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.861731, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-L\u001b[22m,  \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m,  \u001b[1m-U  \u001b[22mand  \u001b[1m-D \u001b[22mmove the visible portion of the window left, right, up or down by \u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m, if the window is larger than the client.  \u001b[1m-c \u001b[22mresets so that the position follows the\u001b[m\r\r\n               cursor.  See the \u001b[1mwindow-size \u001b[22moption.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrename-session \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mnew-name\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mrename\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Rename the session to \u001b[4mnew-name\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mserver-access \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-adlrw\u001b[22m] [\u001b[4muser\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Change the access or read/write permission of \u001b[4muser\u001b[24m.  The user running the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver (its owner) and the root user cannot be changed and are always permitted access.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mare used to give or revoke access for the specified user.  If the user is already attached, the \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mflag causes their clients to be detached.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-w \u001b[22mchange the permissions for \u001b[4muser\u001b[24m: \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mmakes their clients read-only and \u001b[1m-w \u001b[22mwritable.  \u001b[1m-l \u001b[22mlists current access permissions.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               By default, the access list is empty and \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mcreates sockets with file system permissions preventing access by any user other than the owner (and root).   These  permissions  must  be\u001b[m\r\r\n               changed manually.  Great care should be taken not to allow access to untrusted users even read-only.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mshow-messages \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-JT\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-client\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mshowmsgs\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Show  server  messages  or  information.  Messages are stored, up to a maximum of the limit set by the \u001b[4mmessage-limit\u001b[24m server option.  \u001b[1m-J \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22mshow debugging information about jobs and\u001b[m\r\r\n               terminals.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msource-file \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-Fnqv\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mpath\u001b[24m \u001b[4m...\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1msource\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Execute commands from one or more files specified by \u001b[4mpath\u001b[24m (which may be \u001b[4mglob\u001b[24m(7) patterns).  If \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mis present, then \u001b[4mpath\u001b[24m is expanded as a format.  If \u001b[1m-q \u001b[22mis given, no error will  be  re‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               turned if \u001b[4mpath\u001b[24m does not exist.  With \u001b[1m-n\u001b[22m, the file is parsed but no commands are executed.  \u001b[1m-v \u001b[22mshows the parsed commands and line numbers if possible.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstart-server\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mstart\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Start the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver, if not already running, without creating any sessions.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               Note  that as by default the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver will exit with no sessions, this is only useful if a session is created in \u001b[4m~/.tmux.conf\u001b[24m, \u001b[1mexit-empty \u001b[22mis turned off, or another command is run as\u001b[m\r\r\n               part of the same command sequence.  For example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     $ tmux start \\; show -g\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msuspend-client \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-client\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1msuspendc\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Suspend a client by sending SIGTSTP (tty stop).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mswitch-client \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-ElnprZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-client\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-T \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey-table\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mswitchc\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Switch the current session for client \u001b[4mtarget-client\u001b[24m to \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m.  As a special case, \u001b[1m-t \u001b[22mmay refer to a pane (a target that contains ‘:’, ‘.’ or ‘%’), to  change  session,  window\u001b[m\r\r\n               and  pane.  In that case, \u001b[1m-Z \u001b[22mkeeps the window zoomed if it was zoomed.  If \u001b[1m-l\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m-n \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-p \u001b[22mis used, the client is moved to the last, next or previous session respectively.  \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mtoggles the\u001b[m\r\r\n               c"]
[1.862161, "o", "lient \u001b[1mread-only \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mignore-size \u001b[22mflags (see the \u001b[1mattach-session \u001b[22mcommand).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1m-E \u001b[22mis used, \u001b[1mupdate-environment \u001b[22moption will not be applied.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 540 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[1.942818, "o", "\r\u001b[K               \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22msets the client's key table; the next key from the client will be interpreted from \u001b[4mkey-table\u001b[24m.  This may be used to configure multiple prefix keys, or to bind commands  to  sequences\u001b[m\r\r\n               of keys.  For example, to make typing ‘abc’ run the \u001b[1mlist-keys \u001b[22mcommand:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     bind-key -Ttable2 c list-keys\u001b[m\r\r\n                     bind-key -Ttable1 b switch-client -Ttable2\u001b[m\r\r\n                     bind-key -Troot   a switch-client -Ttable1\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mWINDOWS AND PANES\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       Each  window  displayed  by  \u001b[1mtmux  \u001b[22mmay be split into one or more \u001b[4mpanes\u001b[24m; each pane takes up a certain area of the display and is a separate terminal.  A window may be split into panes using the\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msplit-window \u001b[22mcommand.  Windows may be split horizontally (with the \u001b[1m-h \u001b[22mflag) or vertically.  Panes may be resized with the \u001b[1mresize-pane \u001b[22mcommand (bound to ‘C-Up’, ‘C-Down’ ‘C-Left’ and  ‘C-Right’\u001b[m\r\r\n       by  default),  the  current  pane may be changed with the \u001b[1mselect-pane \u001b[22mcommand and the \u001b[1mrotate-window \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mswap-pane \u001b[22mcommands may be used to swap panes without changing their position.  Panes are\u001b[m\r\r\n       numbered beginning from zero in the order they are created.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       By default, a \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mpane permits direct access to the terminal contained in the pane.  A pane may also be put into one of several modes:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mCopy mode, which permits a section of a window or its history to be copied to a \u001b[4mpaste\u001b[24m \u001b[4mbuffer\u001b[24m for later insertion into another window.  This mode is entered with  the  \u001b[1mcopy-mode  \u001b[22mcom‐\u001b[m\r\r\n                 mand, bound to ‘[’ by default.  Copied text can be pasted with the \u001b[1mpaste-buffer \u001b[22mcommand, bound to ‘]’.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mView mode, which is like copy mode but is entered when a command that produces output, such as \u001b[1mlist-keys\u001b[22m, is executed from a key binding.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n   "]
[1.942935, "o", "          \u001b[1m-   \u001b[22mChoose  mode,  which  allows  an  item  to  be  chosen  from  a  list.   This may be a client, a session or window or pane, or a buffer.  This mode is entered with the \u001b[1mchoose-buffer\u001b[22m,\u001b[m\r\r\n                 \u001b[1mchoose-client \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mchoose-tree \u001b[22mcommands.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       In copy mode an indicator is displayed in the top-right corner of the pane with the current position and the number of lines in the history.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Commands are sent to copy mode using the \u001b[1m-X \u001b[22mflag to the \u001b[1msend-keys \u001b[22mcommand.  When a key is pressed, copy mode automatically uses one of two  key  tables,  depending  on  the  \u001b[1mmode-keys  \u001b[22moption:\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-mode \u001b[22mfor emacs, or \u001b[1mcopy-mode-vi \u001b[22mfor vi.  Key tables may be viewed with the \u001b[1mlist-keys \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The following commands are supported in copy mode:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mappend-selection\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Append the selection to the top paste buffer.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mappend-selection-an"]
[1.943236, "o", "d-cancel \u001b[22m(vi: A)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Append the selection to the top paste buffer and exit copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mback-to-indentation \u001b[22m(vi: ^) (emacs: M-m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move the cursor back to the indentation.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mbegin-selection \u001b[22m(vi: Space) (emacs: C-Space)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Begin selection.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mbottom-line \u001b[22m(vi: L)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the bottom line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcancel \u001b[22m(vi: q) (emacs: Escape)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Exit copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mclear-selection \u001b[22m(vi: Escape) (emacs: C-g)\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 589 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.025825, "o", "\r\u001b[K               Clear the current selection.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-end-of-line \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Copy from the cursor position to the end of the line.  \u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m is used to name the new paste buffer.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-end-of-line-and-cancel \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Copy from the cursor position and exit copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-pipe-end-of-line \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Copy from the cursor position to the end of the line and pipe the text to \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m.  \u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m is used to name the new paste buffer.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-pipe-end-of-line-and-cancel \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mcopy-pipe-end-of-line \u001b[22mbut also exit copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-line \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Copy the entire line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-line-and-cancel \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Copy the entire line and exit copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-"]
[2.026008, "o", "pipe-line \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Copy the entire line and pipe the text to \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m.  \u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m is used to name the new paste buffer.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-pipe-line-and-cancel \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mcopy-pipe-line \u001b[22mbut also exit copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-pipe \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Copy the selection, clear it and pipe its text to \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m.  \u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m is used to name the new paste buffer.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-pipe-no-clear \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mcopy-pipe \u001b[22mbut do not clear the selection.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-pipe-and-cancel \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mcopy-pipe \u001b[22mbut also exit copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-selection \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Copies the current selection.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-selection-no-clear \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mcopy-selection \u001b[22mbut do not clear the selection.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-selection-and-cancel \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m] (vi: Enter) (emacs: M-w)\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[2.026152, "o", "               Copy the current selection and exit copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcursor-down \u001b[22m(vi: j) (emacs: Down)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move the cursor down.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcursor-down-and-cancel\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mcursor-down \u001b[22mbut also exit copy mode if reaching the bottom.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 638 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.116133, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcursor-left \u001b[22m(vi: h) (emacs: Left)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move the cursor left.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcursor-right \u001b[22m(vi: l) (emacs: Right)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move the cursor right.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcursor-up \u001b[22m(vi: k) (emacs: Up)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move the cursor up.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mend-of-line \u001b[22m(vi: $) (emacs: C-e)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move the cursor to the end of the line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mgoto-line \u001b[4m\u001b[22mline\u001b[24m (vi: :) (emacs: g)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move the cursor to a specific line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mhalfpage-down \u001b[22m(vi: C-d) (emacs: M-Down)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll down by half a page.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mhalfpage-down-and-cancel\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mhalfpage-down \u001b[22mbut also exit copy mode if reaching the bottom.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mhalfpage-up \u001b[22m(vi: C-u) (emacs: M-Up)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll up by half a page.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mhistory-bottom \u001b[22m(vi: G) (emacs: M->)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll to the bottom of the history.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mhistory-top \u001b[22"]
[2.116322, "o", "m(vi: g) (emacs: M-<)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll to the top of the history.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mjump-again \u001b[22m(vi: ;) (emacs: ;)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Repeat the last jump.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mjump-backward \u001b[4m\u001b[22mto\u001b[24m (vi: F) (emacs: F)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Jump backwards to the specified text.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mjump-forward \u001b[4m\u001b[22mto\u001b[24m (vi: f) (emacs: f)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Jump forward to the specified text.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mjump-reverse \u001b[22m(vi: ,) (emacs: ,)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Repeat the last jump in the reverse direction (forward becomes backward and backward becomes forward).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mjump-to-backward \u001b[4m\u001b[22mto\u001b[24m (vi: T)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Jump backwards, but one character less, placing the cursor on the character after the target.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mjump-to-forward \u001b[4m\u001b[22mto\u001b[24m (vi: t)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Jump forward, but one character less, placing the cursor on the character before the target.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 687 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.204512, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[2.204658, "o", "       \u001b[1mjump-to-mark \u001b[22m(vi: M-x) (emacs: M-x)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Jump to the last mark.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmiddle-line \u001b[22m(vi: M) (emacs: M-r)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the middle line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnext-matching-bracket \u001b[22m(vi: %) (emacs: M-C-f)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the next matching bracket.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnext-paragraph \u001b[22m(vi: }) (emacs: M-})\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the next paragraph.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnext-prompt \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-o\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the next prompt.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnext-word \u001b[22m(vi: w)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the next word.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnext-word-end \u001b[22m(vi: e) (emacs: M-f)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the end of the next word.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnext-space \u001b[22m(vi: W)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mnext-word \u001b[22mbut use a space alone as the word separator.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnext-space-end \u001b[22m(vi: E)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mnext-word-end \u001b[22mbut use a space alone as the word separator.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mother-end \u001b[22m(vi: o)\u001b[m\r\r\n     "]
[2.205665, "o", "          Switch at which end of the selection the cursor sits.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpage-down \u001b[22m(vi: C-f) (emacs: PageDown)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll down by one page.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpage-down-and-cancel\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mpage-down \u001b[22mbut also exit copy mode if reaching the bottom.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpage-up \u001b[22m(vi: C-b) (emacs: PageUp)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll up by one page.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpipe \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Pipe the selected text to \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m and clear the selection.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpipe-no-clear \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mpipe \u001b[22mbut do not clear the selection.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpipe-and-cancel \u001b[22m[\u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mpipe \u001b[22mbut also exit copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprevious-matching-bracket \u001b[22m(emacs: M-C-b)\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 736 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.288535, "o", "\r\u001b[K               Move to the previous matching bracket.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprevious-paragraph \u001b[22m(vi: {) (emacs: M-{)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the previous paragraph.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprevious-prompt \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-o\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the previous prompt.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprevious-word \u001b[22m(vi: b) (emacs: M-b)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the previous word.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprevious-space \u001b[22m(vi: B)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mprevious-word \u001b[22mbut use a space alone as the word separator.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrectangle-on\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Turn on rectangle selection mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrectangle-off\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Turn off rectangle selection mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrectangle-toggle \u001b[22m(vi: v) (emacs: R)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Toggle rectangle selection mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrefresh-from-pane \u001b[22m(vi: r) (emacs: r)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Refresh the content from the pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mscroll-bottom\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll up until the current line is at the bottom while keep"]
[2.288917, "o", "ing the cursor on that line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mscroll-down \u001b[22m(vi: C-e) (emacs: C-Down)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll down.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mscroll-down-and-cancel\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Same as \u001b[1mscroll-down \u001b[22mbut also exit copy mode if the cursor reaches the bottom.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mscroll-middle \u001b[22m(vi: z)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll so that the current line becomes the middle one while keeping the cursor on that line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mscroll-top\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll down until the current line is at the top while keeping the cursor on that line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mscroll-up \u001b[22m(vi: C-y) (emacs: C-Up)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Scroll up.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msearch-again \u001b[22m(vi: n) (emacs: n)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Repeat the last search.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msearch-backward \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtext\u001b[24m (vi: ?)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Search backwards for the specified text.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 785 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.377864, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msearch-backward-incremental \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtext\u001b[24m (emacs: C-r)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Search backwards incrementally for the specified text.  Is expected to be used with the \u001b[1m-i \u001b[22mflag to the \u001b[1mcommand-prompt \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msearch-backward-text \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtext\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Search backwards for the specified plain text.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msearch-forward \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtext\u001b[24m (vi: /)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Search forward for the specified text.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msearch-forward-incremental \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtext\u001b[24m (emacs: C-s)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Search forward incrementally for the specified text.  Is expected to be used with the \u001b[1m-i \u001b[22mflag to the \u001b[1mcommand-prompt \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msearch-forward-text \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtext\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Search forward for the specified plain text.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msearch-reverse \u001b[22m(vi: N) (emacs: N)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Repeat the last search in the reverse direction (forward becomes backward and backward becomes forward).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n  "]
[2.378211, "o", "     \u001b[1mselect-line \u001b[22m(vi: V)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Select the current line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mselect-word\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Select the current word.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mset-mark \u001b[22m(vi: X) (emacs: X)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Mark the current line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstart-of-line \u001b[22m(vi: 0) (emacs: C-a)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move the cursor to the start of the line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstop-selection\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Stop selecting without clearing the current selection.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtoggle-position \u001b[22m(vi: P) (emacs: P)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Toggle the visibility of the position indicator in the top right.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtop-line \u001b[22m(vi: H) (emacs: M-R)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the top line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The search commands come in several varieties: ‘search-forward’ and ‘search-backward’ search for a regular expression; the ‘-text’ variants search for a plain text string rather than a regular\u001b[m\r\r\n       expression; ‘-incremental’ perform an incremental search and expect to be used with the \u001b[1m-i \u001b[22mflag to the \u001b[1mcommand-prompt \u001b[22mcommand.  ‘search-again’ repeats the last search and ‘search-reverse’ does\u001b[m\r\r\n       the same but reverses the direction (forward becomes backward and backward becomes forward).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The ‘next-prompt’ and ‘previous-prompt’ move between shell prompts, but require the shell to emit an escape sequence (\\033]133;A\\033\\\\) to tell \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwhere the prompts are located; if the shell\u001b[m\r\r\n       does not do this, these commands will do nothing.  The \u001b[1m-o \u001b[22mflag jumps to the beginning of the command output instead of the shell prompt.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Copy  commands  may  take an optional buffer prefix argument which is used to generate the buffer name (the default is ‘buffer’ so buffers are named ‘buffer0’, ‘buffer1’ and so on).  Pipe com‐\u001b[m\r\r\n       mands take a command argument which is the command to which the selected text is piped.  ‘copy-pipe’ variants also copy the selection.  The ‘-and-cancel’ variants of some  commands  exit  copy\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 834 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.462039, "o", "\r\u001b[K       mode after they have completed (for copy commands) or when the cursor reaches the bottom (for scrolling commands).  ‘-no-clear’ variants do not clear the selection.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The next and previous word keys skip over whitespace and treat consecutive runs of either word separators or other letters as words.  Word separators can be customized with the \u001b[4mword-separators\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       session  option.   Next  word  moves  to the start of the next word, next word end to the end of the next word and previous word to the start of the previous word.  The three next and previous\u001b[m\r\r\n       space keys work similarly but use a space alone as the word separator.  Setting \u001b[4mword-separators\u001b[24m to the empty string makes next/previous word equivalent to next/previous space.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The jump commands enable quick movement within a line.  For instance, typing ‘f’ followed by ‘/’ will move the cursor to the next ‘/’ character on the current line.  A ‘;’ will  then  jump  to\u001b[m\r\r\n       the next occurrence.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Commands  in  copy mode may be prefaced by an optional repeat count.  With vi key bindings, a prefix is entered using the number keys; with emacs, the Alt (meta) key and a number begins prefix\u001b[m\r\r\n       entry.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The synopsis for the \u001b[1mcopy-mode \u001b[22mcommand is:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-mode \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-deHMqu\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22msrc-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Enter copy mode.  \u001b[1m-u \u001b[22malso scrolls one page up after entering and \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mone page down if already in copy mode.  \u001b[1m-M \u001b[22mbegins a mouse drag (only valid if bound to  a  mouse  key  binding,  see\u001b[m\r\r\n               “MOUSE SUPPORT”).  \u001b[1m-H \u001b[22mhides the position indicator in the top right.  \u001b[1m-q \u001b[22mcancels copy mode and any other modes.  \u001b[1m-s \u001b[22mcopies from \u001b[4msrc-pane\u001b[24m instead of \u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-e  \u001b[22mspecifies  that  scrolling  to the bottom of the history (to the visible screen) should exit copy mod"]
[2.46259, "o", "e.  While in copy mode, pressing a key other than those used for scrolling will\u001b[m\r\r\n               disable this behaviour.  This is intended to allow fast scrolling through a pane's history, for example with:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     bind PageUp copy-mode -eu\u001b[m\r\r\n                     bind PageDown copy-mode -ed\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       A number of preset arrangements of panes are available, these are called layouts.  These may be selected with the \u001b[1mselect-layout \u001b[22mcommand or cycled with \u001b[1mnext-layout  \u001b[22m(bound  to  ‘Space’  by  de‐\u001b[m\r\r\n       fault); once a layout is chosen, panes within it may be moved and resized as normal.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The following layouts are supported:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1meven-horizontal\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Panes are spread out evenly from left to right across the window.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1meven-vertical\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Panes are spread evenly from top to bottom.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmain-horizontal\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               A large (main) pane is shown at the top of the window and the remaining panes are spread from left to right in the leftover space at the bottom.  Use the \u001b[4mmain-pane-height\u001b[24m window option\u001b[m\r\r\n               to specify the height of the top pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmain-horizontal-mirrored\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               The same as \u001b[1mmain-horizontal \u001b[22mbut mirrored so the main pane is at the bottom of the window.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmain-vertical\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               A  large (main) pane is shown on the left of the window and the remaining panes are spread from top to bottom in the leftover space on the right.  Use the \u001b[4mmain-pane-width\u001b[24m window option\u001b[m\r\r\n               to specify the width of the left pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmain-vertical-mirrored\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               The same as \u001b[1mmain-vertical \u001b[22mbut mirrored so the main pane is on the right of the window.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 883 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.547072, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[2.547233, "o", "       \u001b[1mtiled   \u001b[22mPanes are spread out as evenly as possible over the window in both rows and columns.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       In addition, \u001b[1mselect-layout \u001b[22mmay be used to apply a previously used layout - the \u001b[1mlist-windows \u001b[22mcommand displays the layout of each window in a form suitable for use with \u001b[1mselect-layout\u001b[22m.  For exam‐\u001b[m\r\r\n       ple:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux list-windows\u001b[m\r\r\n             0: ksh [159x48]\u001b[m\r\r\n                 layout: bb62,159x48,0,0{79x48,0,0,79x48,80,0}\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux select-layout 'bb62,159x48,0,0{79x48,0,0,79x48,80,0}'\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mautomatically adjusts the size of the layout for the current window size.  Note that a layout cannot be applied to a window with more panes than that from which the layout was  originally\u001b[m\r\r\n       defined.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Commands related to windows and panes are as follows:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mbreak-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-abdP\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-n \u001b[4m\u001b[22mwindow-name\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22msrc-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mdst-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mbreakp\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Break  \u001b[4msrc-pane\u001b[24m off from its containing window to make it the only pane in \u001b[4mdst-window\u001b[24m.  With \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-b\u001b[22m, the window is moved to the next index after or before (existing windows are moved\u001b[m\r\r\n               if necessary).  If \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mis given, the new window does not become the current window.  The \u001b[1m-P \u001b[22moption prints information about the new window after it has been  created.   By  default,  it\u001b[m\r\r\n               uses the format ‘#{session_name}:#{window_index}.#{pane_index}’ but a different format may be specified with \u001b[1m-F\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcapture-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-aAepPqCJN\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-b \u001b[4m\u001b[22mbuffer-name\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-E \u001b[4m\u001b[22mend-line\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-S \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstart-line\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mcapturep\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Capture  the contents of a pane.  If \u001b[1m-p \u001b[22mis given, the output goes to stdout, otherwise to the buffer specified with \u001b[1m-b \u001b[22mor a new buffer if omitted.  If \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mis given, the alternate screen\u001b[m\r\r\n               is used, and the history is not accessible.  If no alternate screen exists, an error will be returned unless \u001b[1m-q \u001b[22mis given.  If \u001b[1m-e \u001b[22mis given, the output includes escape sequences for text\u001b[m\r\r\n               and background attributes.  \u001b[1m-C \u001b[22malso escapes non-printable characters as octal \\xxx.  \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22mignores trailing positions that do not contain a character.  \u001b[1m-N  \u001b[22mpreserves  trailing  spaces  at\u001b[m\r\r\n               each  line's end and \u001b[1m-J \u001b[22mpreserves trailing spaces and joins any wrapped lines; \u001b[1m-J \u001b[22mimplies \u001b[1m-T\u001b[22m.  \u001b[1m-P \u001b[22mcaptures only any output that the pane has received that is the beginning of an as-yet\u001b[m\r\r\n               incomplete escape sequence.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-S \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-E \u001b[22mspecify the starting and ending line numbers, zero is the first line of the visible pane and ne"]
[2.547305, "o", "gative numbers are lines in the history.  ‘-’ to \u001b[1m-S \u001b[22mis the start of the history\u001b[m\r\r\n               and to \u001b[1m-E \u001b[22mthe end of the visible pane.  The default is to capture only the visible contents of the pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mchoose-client \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-NrZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfilter\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-K \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey-format\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-O \u001b[4m\u001b[22msort-order\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mtemplate\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Put a pane into client mode, allowing a client to be selected interactively from a list.  Each client is shown on one line.  A shortcut key is shown on the left  in  brackets  allowing\u001b[m\r\r\n               for immediate choice, or the list may be navigated and an item chosen or otherwise manipulated using the keys below.  \u001b[1m-Z \u001b[22mzooms the pane.  The following keys may be used in client mode:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mKey    Function\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mEnter  \u001b[22mChoose selected client\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mUp     \u001b[22mSelect previous client\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mDown   \u001b[22mSelect next client\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[2.547525, "o", "                     \u001b[1mC-s    \u001b[22mSearch by name\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mn      \u001b[22mRepeat last search forwards\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mN      \u001b[22mRepeat last search backwards\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mt      \u001b[22mToggle if client is tagged\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mT      \u001b[22mTag no clients\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mC-t    \u001b[22mTag all clients\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1md      \u001b[22mDetach selected client\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mD      \u001b[22mDetach tagged clients\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mx      \u001b[22mDetach and HUP selected client\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 932 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.634069, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[2.634236, "o", "                     \u001b[1mX      \u001b[22mDetach and HUP tagged clients\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mz      \u001b[22mSuspend selected client\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mZ      \u001b[22mSuspend tagged clients\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mf      \u001b[22mEnter a format to filter items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mO      \u001b[22mChange sort field\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mr      \u001b[22mReverse sort order\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mv      \u001b[22mToggle preview\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mq      \u001b[22mExit mode\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               After a client is chosen, ‘%%’ is replaced by the client name in \u001b[4mtemplate\u001b[24m and the result executed as a command.  If \u001b[4mtemplate\u001b[24m is not given, \"detach-client -t '%%'\" is used.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-O  \u001b[22mspecifies the initial sort field: one of ‘name’, ‘size’, ‘creation’ (time), or ‘activity’ (time).  \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mreverses the sort order.  \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22mspecifies an initial filter: the filter is a for‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               mat - if it evaluates to zero, the item in the list is not shown, otherwise it is shown.  If a filter would lead to an empty list, it is ignored.  \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mspecifies the format for each item\u001b[m\r\r\n               in the list and \u001b[1m-K \u001b[22ma format for each shortcut key; both are evaluated once for each line.  \u001b[1m-N \u001b[22mstarts without the preview.  This command works only if at least one client is attached.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mchoose-tree \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-GNrswZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfilter\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-K \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey-format\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-O \u001b[4m\u001b[22msort-order\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mtemplate\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Put a pane into tree mode, where a session, window or pane may be chosen interactively from a tree.  Each session, window or pane is shown on one line.  A shortcut key is shown on  the\u001b[m\r\r\n               left in brackets allowing for immediate choice, or the tree may be navigated and an item chosen or otherwise manipulated using the keys below.  \u001b[1m-s \u001b[22mstarts with sessions collapsed and \u001b[1m-w\u001b[0m\u001b"]
[2.634723, "o", "[m\r\r\n               with windows collapsed.  \u001b[1m-Z \u001b[22mzooms the pane.  The following keys may be used in tree mode:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mKey    Function\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mEnter  \u001b[22mChoose selected item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mUp     \u001b[22mSelect previous item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mDown   \u001b[22mSelect next item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1m+      \u001b[22mExpand selected item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1m-      \u001b[22mCollapse selected item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mM-+    \u001b[22mExpand all items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mM--    \u001b[22mCollapse all items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mx      \u001b[22mKill selected item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mX      \u001b[22mKill tagged items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1m<      \u001b[22mScroll list of previews left\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1m>      \u001b[22mScroll list of previews right\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mC-s    \u001b[22mSearch by name\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mm      \u001b[22mSet the marked pane\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mM      \u001b[22mClear the marked pane\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mn      \u001b[22mRepeat last search forwards\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mN      \u001b[22mRepeat last search backwards\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mt      \u001b[22mToggle if item is tagged\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mT      \u001b[22mTag no items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mC-t    \u001b[22mTag all items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1m:      \u001b[22mRun a command for each tagged item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mf      \u001b[22mEnter a format to filter items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mH      \u001b[22mJump to the starting pane\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mO      \u001b[22mChange sort field\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mr      \u001b[22mReverse sort order\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mv      \u001b[22mToggle preview\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mq      \u001b[22mExit mode\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               After  a  session,  window  or  pane  is  chosen,  the first instance of ‘%%’ and all instances of ‘%1’ are replaced by the target in \u001b[4mtemplate\u001b[24m and the result executed as a command.  If\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 981 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.718568, "o", "\r\u001b[K               \u001b[4mtemplate\u001b[24m is not given, \"switch-client -t '%%'\" is used.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-O \u001b[22mspecifies the initial sort field: one of ‘index’, ‘name’, or ‘time’ (activity).  \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mreverses the sort order.  \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22mspecifies an initial filter: the filter is a format - if  it  evalu‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               ates  to zero, the item in the list is not shown, otherwise it is shown.  If a filter would lead to an empty list, it is ignored.  \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mspecifies the format for each item in the tree and\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-K \u001b[22ma format for each shortcut key; both are evaluated once for each line.  \u001b[1m-N \u001b[22mstarts without the preview.  \u001b[1m-G \u001b[22mincludes all sessions in any session groups in the tree rather  than  only\u001b[m\r\r\n               the first.  This command works only if at least one client is attached.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcustomize-mode \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-NZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfilter\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mtemplate\u001b["]
[2.718639, "o", "24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Put  a pane into customize mode, where options and key bindings may be browsed and modified from a list.  Option values in the list are shown for the active pane in the current window.\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-Z \u001b[22mzooms the pane.  The following keys may be used in customize mode:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mKey    Function\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mEnter  \u001b[22mSet pane, window, session or global option value\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mUp     \u001b[22mSelect previous item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mDown   \u001b[22mSelect next item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1m+      \u001b[22mExpand selected item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1m-      \u001b[22mCollapse selected item\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mM-+    \u001b[22mExpand all items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mM--    \u001b[22mCollapse all items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1ms      \u001b[22mSet option value or key attribute\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mS      \u001b[22mSet global option value\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mw      \u001b[22mSet window option value, if option is for"]
[2.718847, "o", " pane and window\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1md      \u001b[22mSet an option or key to the default\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mD      \u001b[22mSet tagged options and tagged keys to the default\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mu      \u001b[22mUnset an option (set to default value if global) or unbind a key\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mU      \u001b[22mUnset tagged options and unbind tagged keys\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mC-s    \u001b[22mSearch by name\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mn      \u001b[22mRepeat last search forwards\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mN      \u001b[22mRepeat last search backwards\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mt      \u001b[22mToggle if item is tagged\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mT      \u001b[22mTag no items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mC-t    \u001b[22mTag all items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mf      \u001b[22mEnter a format to filter items\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mv      \u001b[22mToggle option information\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mq      \u001b[22mExit mode\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22mspecifies an initial filter: the filter is a format - if it evalua"]
[2.718996, "o", "tes to zero, the item in the list is not shown, otherwise it is shown.  If a filter would lead to an empty list, it\u001b[m\r\r\n               is ignored.  \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mspecifies the format for each item in the tree.  \u001b[1m-N \u001b[22mstarts without the option information.  This command works only if at least one client is attached.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdisplay-panes \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-bN\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-d \u001b[4m\u001b[22mduration\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-client\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mtemplate\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mdisplayp\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Display a visible indicator of each pane shown by \u001b[4mtarget-client\u001b[24m.  See the \u001b[1mdisplay-panes-colour \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mdisplay-panes-active-colour \u001b[22msession options.  The indicator is closed when a  key  is\u001b[m\r\r\n               pressed  (unless  \u001b[1m-N  \u001b[22mis  given)  or  \u001b[4mduration\u001b[24m  milliseconds  have passed.  If \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mis not given, \u001b[1mdisplay-panes-time \u001b[22mis used.  A duration of zero means the indicator stays until a key is\u001b[m\r\r\n               pressed.  While the indicator is on screen, a pane may be chosen with the ‘0’ to ‘9’ keys, which will cause \u001b[4mtemplate\u001b[24m to be executed as a command with ‘%%’ substituted by the  pane  ID.\u001b[m\r\r\n               The default \u001b[4mtemplate\u001b[24m is \"select-pane -t '%%'\".  With \u001b[1m-b\u001b[22m, other commands are not blocked from running until the indicator is closed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mfind-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-iCNrTZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mmatch-string\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mfindw\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Search  for  a  \u001b[4mfnmatch\u001b[24m(3) pattern or, with \u001b[1m-r\u001b[22m, regular expression \u001b[4mmatch-string\u001b[24m in window names, titles, and visible content (but not history).  The flags control matching behavior: \u001b[1m-C\u001b[0m"]
[2.719146, "o", "\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1030 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.801835, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[2.802018, "o", "               matches only visible window contents, \u001b[1m-N \u001b[22mmatches only the window name and \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22mmatches only the window title.  \u001b[1m-i \u001b[22mmakes the search ignore case.  The default is \u001b[1m-CNT\u001b[22m.  \u001b[1m-Z \u001b[22mzooms the pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               This command works only if at least one client is attached.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mjoin-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-bdfhv\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-l \u001b[4m\u001b[22msize\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22msrc-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mdst-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mjoinp\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Like \u001b[1msplit-window\u001b[22m, but instead of splitting \u001b[4mdst-pane\u001b[24m and creating a new pane, split it and move \u001b[4msrc-pane\u001b[24m into the space.  This can be used to reverse \u001b[1mbreak-pane\u001b[22m.  The \u001b[1m-b \u001b[22moption  causes\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[4msrc-pane\u001b[24m to be joined to left of or above \u001b[4mdst-pane\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1m-s \u001b[22mis omitted and a marked pane is present (see \u001b[1mselect-pane -m\u001b[22m), the marked pane is used rather than the current pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mkill-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-a\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mkillp\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Destroy the given pane.  If no panes remain in the containing window, it is also destroyed.  The \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22moption kills all but the pane given with \u001b[1m-t\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mkill-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-a\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mkillw\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Kill the current window or the window at \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m, removing it from any sessions to which it is linked.  The \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22moption kills all but the window given with \u001b[1m-t\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlast-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-deZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlastp\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Select the last (previously selected) pane.  \u001b[1m-Z \u001b[22mkeeps the window zoomed if it was zoomed.  \u001b[1m-e \u001b[22menables or \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mdisables input to the pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlast-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-ses"]
[2.802045, "o", "sion\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlast\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Select the last (previously selected) window.  If no \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m is specified, select the last window of the current session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlink-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-abdk\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22msrc-window\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mdst-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlinkw\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Link  the window at \u001b[4msrc-window\u001b[24m to the specified \u001b[4mdst-window\u001b[24m.  If \u001b[4mdst-window\u001b[24m is specified and no such window exists, the \u001b[4msrc-window\u001b[24m is linked there.  With \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-b \u001b[22mthe window is moved to\u001b[m\r\r\n               the next index after or before \u001b[4mdst-window\u001b[24m (existing windows are moved if necessary).  If \u001b[1m-k \u001b[22mis given and \u001b[4mdst-window\u001b[24m exists, it is killed, otherwise an error is  generated.   If  \u001b[1m-d  \u001b[22mis\u001b[m\r\r\n               given, the newly linked window is not selected.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlist-panes \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-as\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] "]
[2.802571, "o", "[\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfilter\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlsp\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               If  \u001b[1m-a  \u001b[22mis  given, \u001b[4mtarget\u001b[24m is ignored and all panes on the server are listed.  If \u001b[1m-s \u001b[22mis given, \u001b[4mtarget\u001b[24m is a session (or the current session).  If neither is given, \u001b[4mtarget\u001b[24m is a window (or\u001b[m\r\r\n               the current window).  \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mspecifies the format of each line and \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22ma filter.  Only panes for which the filter is true are shown.  See the “FORMATS” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlist-windows \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-a\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-f \u001b[4m\u001b[22mfilter\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlsw\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mis given, list all windows on the server.  Otherwise, list windows in the current session or in \u001b[4mtarget-session\u001b[24m.  \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mspecifies the format of each line and \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22ma filter.  Only  win‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               dows for which the filter is true are shown.  See the “FORMATS” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmove-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-bdfhv\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-l \u001b[4m\u001b[22msize\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22msrc-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mdst-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mmovep\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Does the same as \u001b[1mjoin-pane\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmove-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-abrdk\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22msrc-window\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mdst-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mmovew\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1079 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.888226, "o", "\r\u001b[K               This  is  similar  to  \u001b[1mlink-window\u001b[22m,  except  the  window  at  \u001b[4msrc-window\u001b[24m is moved to \u001b[4mdst-window\u001b[24m.  With \u001b[1m-r\u001b[22m, all windows in the session are renumbered in sequential order, respecting the\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mbase-index \u001b[22moption.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnew-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-abdkPS\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstart-directory\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-e \u001b[4m\u001b[22menvironment\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-n \u001b[4m\u001b[22mwindow-name\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mneww\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Create a new window.  With \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-b\u001b[22m, the new window is inserted at the next index after or before the specified \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m, moving windows up if necessary;  otherwise  \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               is the new window location.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If  \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mis given, the session does not make the new window the current window.  \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m represents the window t"]
[2.888888, "o", "o be created; if the target already exists an error is shown, unless\u001b[m\r\r\n               the \u001b[1m-k \u001b[22mflag is used, in which case it is destroyed.  If \u001b[1m-S \u001b[22mis given and a window named \u001b[4mwindow-name\u001b[24m already exists, it is selected (unless \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mis also given in  which  case  the  command\u001b[m\r\r\n               does nothing).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m  is the command to execute.  If \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m is not specified, the value of the \u001b[1mdefault-command \u001b[22moption is used.  \u001b[1m-c \u001b[22mspecifies the working directory in which the new win‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               dow is created.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               When the shell command completes, the window closes.  See the \u001b[1mremain-on-exit \u001b[22moption to change this behaviour.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-e \u001b[22mtakes the form ‘VARIABLE=value’ and sets an environment variable for the newly created window; it may be specified multiple times.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The TERM environment variable must be set to ‘screen’ or ‘tmux’ for all programs running \u001b[4minside\u001b[24m \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m.  New windows will automatically have ‘TERM=screen’ added to their environment, but\u001b[m\r\r\n               care must be taken not to reset this in shell start-up files or by the \u001b[1m-e \u001b[22moption.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The \u001b[1m-P \u001b[22moption prints information about the new window after it has been created.  By default, it uses the format ‘#{session_name}:#{window_index}’ but a different format may be  speci‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               fied with \u001b[1m-F\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnext-layout \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mnextl\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move a window to the next layout and rearrange the panes to fit.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mnext-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-a\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mnext\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the next window in the session.  If \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mis used, move to the next window with an alert.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpipe-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-IOo\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mpipep\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Pipe  output  sent  by  the  program  in  \u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m  to  a  shell  command  or  vice versa.  A pane may only be connected to one command at a time, any existing pipe is closed before\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m is executed.  The \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m string may contain the special character sequences supported by the \u001b[1mstatus-left \u001b[22moption.  If no \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m is given,  the  current  pipe\u001b[m\r\r\n               (if any) is closed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-I  \u001b[22mand  \u001b[1m-O  \u001b[22mspecify  which of the \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m output streams are connected to the pane: with \u001b[1m-I \u001b[22mstdout is connected (so anything \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m prints is written to the pane as if it\u001b[m\r\r\n   "]
[2.889312, "o", "            were typed); with \u001b[1m-O \u001b[22mstdin is connected (so any output in the pane is piped to \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m).  Both may be used together and if neither are specified, \u001b[1m-O \u001b[22mis used.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The \u001b[1m-o \u001b[22moption only opens a new pipe if no previous pipe exists, allowing a pipe to be toggled with a single key, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     bind-key C-p pipe-pane -o 'cat >>~/output.#I-#P'\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprevious-layout \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mprevl\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the previous layout in the session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1128 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[2.973925, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprevious-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-a\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-session\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mprev\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Move to the previous window in the session.  With \u001b[1m-a\u001b[22m, move to the previous window with an alert.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrename-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mnew-name\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mrenamew\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Rename the current window, or the window at \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m if specified, to \u001b[4mnew-name\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mresize-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-DLMRTUZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-x \u001b[4m\u001b[22mwidth\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-y \u001b[4m\u001b[22mheight\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mresizep\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Resize a pane, up, down, left or right by \u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m with \u001b[1m-U\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m-D\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m-L \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m, or to an absolute size with \u001b[1m-x \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-y\u001b[22m.  The \u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m is given in lines or columns (the default  is  1);  \u001b[1m-x\u001b[0"]
[2.974581, "o", "m\u001b[m\r\r\n               and \u001b[1m-y \u001b[22mmay be a given as a number of lines or columns or followed by ‘%’ for a percentage of the window size (for example ‘-x 10%’).  With \u001b[1m-Z\u001b[22m, the active pane is toggled between zoomed\u001b[m\r\r\n               (occupying the whole of the window) and unzoomed (its normal position in the layout).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-M \u001b[22mbegins mouse resizing (only valid if bound to a mouse key binding, see “MOUSE SUPPORT”).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22mtrims all lines below the current cursor position and moves lines out of the history to replace them.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mresize-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-aADLRU\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-x \u001b[4m\u001b[22mwidth\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-y \u001b[4m\u001b[22mheight\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mresizew\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Resize  a  window, up, down, left or right by \u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m with \u001b[1m-U\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m-D\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m-L \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m, or to an absolute size with \u001b[1m-x \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-y\u001b[22m.  The \u001b[4madjustment\u001b[24m is given in lines or cells (the default is 1).  \u001b[1m-A\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               sets the size of the largest session containing the window; \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mthe size of the smallest.  This command will automatically set \u001b[1mwindow-size \u001b[22mto manual in the window options.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrespawn-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-k\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstart-directory\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-e \u001b[4m\u001b[22menvironment\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mrespawnp\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Reactivate a pane in which the command has exited (see the \u001b[1mremain-on-exit \u001b[22mwindow option).  If \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m is not given, the command used when the pane was created or  last  respawned\u001b[m\r\r\n               is  executed.   The  pane must be already inactive, unless \u001b[1m-k \u001b[22mis given, in which case any existing command is killed.  \u001b[1m-c \u001b[22mspecifies a new working directory for the pane.  The \u001b[1m-e \u001b[22moption\u001b[m\r\r\n               has the same meaning as for the \u001b[1mnew-window \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrespawn-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-k\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstart-directory\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-e \u001b[4m\u001b[22menvironment\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mrespawnw\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Reactivate a window in which the command has exited (see the \u001b[1mremain-on-exit \u001b[22mwindow option).  If \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m is not given, the command  used  when  the  window  was  created  or  last\u001b[m\r\r\n               respawned  is  executed.   The  window must be already inactive, unless \u001b[1m-k \u001b[22mis given, in which case any existing command is killed.  \u001b[1m-c \u001b[22mspecifies a new working directory for the window.\u001b[m\r\r\n               The \u001b[1m-e \u001b[22moption has the same meaning as for the \u001b[1mnew-window \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrotate-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-DUZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mrotatew\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Rotate the positions of the panes within a window, either upward (numerically lower) with \u001b[1m-U \u001b[22mor downward (numerically higher).  \u001b[1m-Z \u001b[22mkeeps the window zoomed if it was zoomed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mselect-layout \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-Enop\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mlayout-name\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mselectl\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Choose a specific layout for a window.  If \u001b[4mlayout-name\u001b[24m is not given, the last preset layout used (if any) is reapplied.  \u001b[1m-n \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-p \u001b[22mare equivalent to the \u001b[1mnext-layout \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mprevious-layout\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               commands.  \u001b[1m-o \u001b[22mapplies the last set layout if possible (undoes the most recent layout change).  \u001b[1m-E \u001b[22mspreads the current pane and any panes next to it out evenly.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mselect-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-DdeLlMmRUZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-T \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtitle\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mselectp\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Make pane \u001b[4mtarge"]
[2.974712, "o", "t-pane\u001b[24m the active pane in its window.  If one of \u001b[1m-D\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m-L\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m, or \u001b[1m-U \u001b[22mis used, respectively the pane below, to the left, to the right, or above the target pane is used.  \u001b[1m-Z\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               keeps the window zoomed if it was zoomed.  \u001b[1m-l \u001b[22mis the same as using the \u001b[1mlast-pane \u001b[22mcommand.  \u001b[1m-e \u001b[22menables or \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mdisables input to the pane.  \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22msets the pane title.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1177 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.055602, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[3.056004, "o", "\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-m \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-M \u001b[22mare used to set and clear the \u001b[4mmarked\u001b[24m \u001b[4mpane\u001b[24m.  There is one marked pane at a time, setting a new marked pane clears the last.  The marked pane is the default target  for  \u001b[1m-s  \u001b[22mto\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mjoin-pane\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mmove-pane\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mswap-pane \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mswap-window\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mselect-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-lnpT\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mselectw\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Select  the  window at \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m.  \u001b[1m-l\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m-n \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-p \u001b[22mare equivalent to the \u001b[1mlast-window\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mnext-window \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mprevious-window \u001b[22mcommands.  If \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22mis given and the selected window is already the\u001b[m\r\r\n               current window, the command behaves like \u001b[1mlast-window\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msplit-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-bdfhIvPZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstart-directory\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-e \u001b[4m\u001b[22menvironment\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-l \u001b[4m\u001b[22msize\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-F \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1msplitw\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Create a new pane by splitting \u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m: \u001b[1m-h \u001b[22mdoes a horizontal split and \u001b[1m-v \u001b[22ma vertical split; if neither is specified, \u001b[1m-v \u001b[22mis assumed.  The \u001b[1m-l \u001b[22moption specifies the  size  of  the  new\u001b[m\r\r\n               pane  in lines (for vertical split) or in columns (for horizontal split); \u001b[4msize\u001b[24m may be followed by ‘%’ to specify a percentage of the available space.  The \u001b[1m-b \u001b[22moption causes the new pane\u001b[m\r\r\n               to be created to the left of or above \u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m.  The \u001b[1m-f \u001b[22moption creates a new pane spanning the full window height (with \u001b[1m-h\u001b[22m) or full window width (with \u001b[1m-v\u001b[22m), instead of splitting  the\u001b[m\r\r\n               active pane.  \u001b[1m-Z \u001b[22mzooms if the window is not zoomed, or keeps it zoomed if already zoomed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               An empty \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m ('') will create a pane with no command running in it.  Output can be sent to such a pane with the \u001b[1mdisplay-message \u001b[22mcommand.  The \u001b[1m-I \u001b[22mflag (if \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m is not\u001b[m\r\r\n               specified or empty) will create an empty pane and forward any output from stdin to it.  For example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     $ make 2>&1|tmux splitw -dI &\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               All other options have the same meaning as for the \u001b[1mnew-window \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mswap-pane \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-dDUZ\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22msrc-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mdst-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mswapp\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Swap two panes.  If \u001b[1m-U \u001b[22mis used and no source pane is specified with \u001b[1m-s\u001b[22m, \u001b[4mdst-pane\u001b[24m is swapped with the previous pane (before it numerically); \u001b[1m-D \u001b[22mswaps with the next pane (after it numer‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               ically).  \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22minstructs \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mnot to change the active pane and \u001b[1m-Z \u001b[22mkeeps the window zoomed if it was zoomed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1m-s \u001b[22mis omitted and a marked pane is present (see \u001b[1mselect-pane -m\u001b[22m), the marked pane is used rather than"]
[3.056056, "o", " the current pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mswap-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-d\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-s \u001b[4m\u001b[22msrc-window\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mdst-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mswapw\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               This  is  similar to \u001b[1mlink-window\u001b[22m, except the source and destination windows are swapped.  It is an error if no window exists at \u001b[4msrc-window\u001b[24m.  If \u001b[1m-d \u001b[22mis given, the new window does not be‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               come the current window.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1m-s \u001b[22mis omitted and a marked pane is present (see \u001b[1mselect-pane -m\u001b[22m), the window containing the marked pane is used rather than the current window.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1munlink-window \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-k\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-window\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1munlinkw\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Unlink \u001b[4mtarget-window\u001b[24m.  Unless \u001b[1m-k \u001b[22mis given, a window may be unlinked only if it is linked to multiple sessions - windows may not be linked to no sessions; if \u001b[1m-k  \u001b[22mis  specified  and  the\u001b[m\r\r\n       "]
[3.056653, "o", "        window is linked to only one session, it is unlinked and destroyed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mKEY BINDINGS\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mallows a command to be bound to most keys, with or without a prefix key.  When specifying keys, most represent themselves (for example ‘A’ to ‘Z’).  Ctrl keys may be prefixed with ‘C-’ or\u001b[m\r\r\n       ‘^’, Shift keys with ‘S-’ and Alt (meta) with ‘M-’.  In addition, the following special key names are accepted: \u001b[4mUp\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mDown\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mLeft\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mRight\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mBSpace\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mBTab\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mDC\u001b[24m (Delete), \u001b[4mEnd\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mEnter\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mEscape\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mF1\u001b[24m to \u001b[4mF12\u001b[24m,\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4mHome\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mIC\u001b[24m (Insert), \u001b[4mNPage/PageDown/PgDn\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mPPage/PageUp/PgUp\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mSpace\u001b[24m, and \u001b[4mTab\u001b[24m.  Note that to bind the ‘\"’ or ‘'’ keys, quotation marks are necessary, for example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             bind-key '\"' split-window\u001b[m\r\r\n             bind-key \"'\" new-window\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1226 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.13826, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n       A command bound to the \u001b[4mAny\u001b[24m key will execute for all keys which do not have a more specific binding.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Commands related to key bindings are as follows:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mbind-key \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-nr\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-N \u001b[4m\u001b[22mnote\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-T \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey-table\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mkey\u001b[24m \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m [\u001b[4margument\u001b[24m \u001b[4m...\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mbind\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Bind  key  \u001b[4mkey\u001b[24m to \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m.  Keys are bound in a key table.  By default (without -T), the key is bound in the \u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m key table.  This table is used for keys pressed after the prefix key\u001b[m\r\r\n               (for example, by default ‘c’ is bound to \u001b[1mnew-window \u001b[22min the \u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m table, so ‘C-b c’ creates a new window).  The \u001b[4mroot\u001b[24m table is used for keys pressed without the prefix key: binding  ‘c’\u001b[m\r\r\n               to  \u001b[1mnew-window  \u001b[22min  the  \u001b[4mroot\u001b[24m  table  (not  recommended)  means  a plain ‘c’ will create a new window.  \u001b[1m-n"]
[3.138451, "o", " \u001b[22mis an alias for \u001b[1m-T \u001b[4m\u001b[22mroot\u001b[24m.  Keys may also be bound in custom key tables and the\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mswitch-client -T \u001b[22mcommand used to switch to them from a key binding.  The \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mflag indicates this key may repeat, see the \u001b[1mrepeat-time \u001b[22moption.  \u001b[1m-N \u001b[22mattaches a note to the key  (shown  with\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mlist-keys -N\u001b[22m).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               To view the default bindings and possible commands, see the \u001b[1mlist-keys \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlist-keys \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-1aN\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-P \u001b[4m\u001b[22mprefix-string\u001b[24m \u001b[1m-T \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey-table\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4mkey\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mlsk\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               List key bindings.  There are two forms: the default lists keys as \u001b[1mbind-key \u001b[22mcommands; \u001b[1m-N \u001b[22mlists only keys with attached notes and shows only the key and note for each key.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               With the default form, all key tables are listed by default.  \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22mlists only keys in \u001b[4mkey-table\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n   "]
[3.139324, "o", "            With  the \u001b[1m-N \u001b[22mform, only keys in the \u001b[4mroot\u001b[24m and \u001b[4mprefix\u001b[24m key tables are listed by default; \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22malso lists only keys in \u001b[4mkey-table\u001b[24m.  \u001b[1m-P \u001b[22mspecifies a prefix to print before each key and \u001b[1m-1 \u001b[22mlists\u001b[m\r\r\n               only the first matching key.  \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mlists the command for keys that do not have a note rather than skipping them.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[3.139638, "o", "       \u001b[1msend-keys \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-FHKlMRX\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-c \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-client\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-N \u001b[4m\u001b[22mrepeat-count\u001b[24m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mkey\u001b[24m \u001b[4m...\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1msend\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Send a key or keys to a window or client.  Each argument \u001b[4mkey\u001b[24m is the name of the key (such as ‘C-a’ or ‘NPage’) to send; if the string is not recognised as a key, it is sent as a series\u001b[m\r\r\n               of characters.  If \u001b[1m-K \u001b[22mis given, keys are sent to \u001b[4mtarget-client\u001b[24m, so they are looked up in the client's key table, rather than to \u001b[4mtarget-pane\u001b[24m.  All arguments are sent  sequentially  from\u001b[m\r\r\n               first to last.  If no keys are given and the command is bound to a key, then that key is used.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The \u001b[1m-l \u001b[22mflag disables key name lookup and processes the keys as literal UTF-8 characters.  The \u001b[1m-H \u001b[22mflag expects each key to be a hexadecimal number for an ASCII character.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The \u001b[1m-R \u001b[22mflag causes the terminal state to be reset.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-M \u001b[22mpasses through a mouse event (only valid if bound to a mouse key binding, see “MOUSE SUPPORT”).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-X \u001b[22mis used to send a command into copy mode - see the “WINDOWS AND PANES” section.  \u001b[1m-N \u001b[22mspecifies a repeat count and \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mexpands formats in arguments where appropriate.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msend-prefix \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-2\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Send the prefix key, or with \u001b[1m-2 \u001b[22mthe secondary prefix key, to a window as if it was pressed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1munbind-key \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-anq\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-T \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey-table\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mkey\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1munbind\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Unbind the command bound to \u001b[4mkey\u001b[24m.  \u001b[1m-n \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-T \u001b[22mare the same as for \u001b[1mbind-key\u001b[22m.  If \u001b[1m-a \u001b[22mis present, all key bindings are removed.  The \u001b[1m-q \u001b[22moption prevents errors being returned.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mOPTIONS\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       The  appearance  and  behaviour  of  \u001b[1mtmux  \u001b[22mmay  be modified by changing the value of various options.  There are four types of option: \u001b[4mserver\u001b[24m \u001b[4moptions\u001b[24m, \u001b[4msession\u001b[24m \u001b[4moptions\u001b[24m, \u001b[4mwindow\u001b[24m \u001b[4moptions\u001b[24m, and \u001b[4mpane\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[4moptions\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1275 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.22077, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[3.220875, "o", "       The \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mserver has a set of global server options which do not apply to any particular window or session or pane.  These are altered with the \u001b[1mset-option -s  \u001b[22mcommand,  or  displayed  with  the\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mshow-options -s \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       In  addition,  each  individual session may have a set of session options, and there is a separate set of global session options.  Sessions which do not have a particular option configured in‐\u001b[m\r\r\n       herit the value from the global session options.  Session options are set or unset with the \u001b[1mset-option \u001b[22mcommand and may be listed with the \u001b[1mshow-options \u001b[22mcommand.  The available server  and  ses‐\u001b[m\r\r\n       sion options are listed under the \u001b[1mset-option \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Similarly,  a  set  of  window options is attached to each window and a set of pane options to each pane.  Pane options inherit from window options.  This means any pane option may be set as a\u001b[m\r\r\n       window option to apply the option to al"]
[3.221254, "o", "l panes in the window without the option set, for example these commands will set the background colour to red for all panes except pane 0:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             set -w window-style bg=red\u001b[m\r\r\n             set -pt:.0 window-style bg=blue\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       There is also a set of global window options from which any unset window or pane options are inherited.  Window and pane options are altered with \u001b[1mset-option -w \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-p  \u001b[22mcommands  and  displayed\u001b[m\r\r\n       with \u001b[1mshow-option -w \u001b[22mand \u001b[1m-p\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22malso supports user options which are prefixed with a ‘@’.  User options may have any name, so long as they are prefixed with ‘@’, and be set to any string.  For example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux set -wq @foo \"abc123\"\u001b[m\r\r\n             $ tmux show -wv @foo\u001b[m\r\r\n             abc123\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Commands which set options are as follows:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mset-option \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-aFgopqsuUw\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] \u001b[4moption\u001b[24m \u001b[4mvalue\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n          "]
[3.22178, "o", "           (alias: \u001b[1mset\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set  a  pane  option with \u001b[1m-p\u001b[22m, a window option with \u001b[1m-w\u001b[22m, a server option with \u001b[1m-s\u001b[22m, otherwise a session option.  If the option is not a user option, \u001b[1m-w \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-s \u001b[22mmay be unnecessary - \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill\u001b[m\r\r\n               infer the type from the option name, assuming \u001b[1m-w \u001b[22mfor pane options.  If \u001b[1m-g \u001b[22mis given, the global session or window option is set.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-F \u001b[22mexpands formats in the option value.  The \u001b[1m-u \u001b[22mflag unsets an option, so a session inherits the option from the global options (or with \u001b[1m-g\u001b[22m, restores a global option to  the  default).\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1m-U  \u001b[22munsets  an  option  (like \u001b[1m-u\u001b[22m) but if the option is a pane option also unsets the option on any panes in the window.  \u001b[4mvalue\u001b[24m depends on the option and may be a number, a string, or a\u001b[m\r\r\n               flag (on, off, or omitted to toggle).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The \u001b[1m-o \u001b[22mflag prevents setting an option that is already set and the \u001b[1m-q \u001b[22mflag suppresses errors about unknown or ambiguous options.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               With \u001b[1m-a\u001b[22m, and if the option expects a string or a style, \u001b[4mvalue\u001b[24m is appended to the existing setting.  For example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     set -g status-left \"foo\"\u001b[m\r\r\n                     set -ag status-left \"bar\"\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               Will result in ‘foobar’.  And:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     set -g status-style \"bg=red\"\u001b[m\r\r\n                     set -ag status-style \"fg=blue\"\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               Will result in a red background \u001b[4mand\u001b[24m blue foreground.  Without \u001b[1m-a\u001b[22m, the result would be the default background and a blue foreground.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mshow-options \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-AgHpqsvw\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] [\u001b[4moption\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n                     (alias: \u001b[1mshow\u001b[22m)\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1324 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.302464, "o", "\r\u001b[K               Show the pane options (or a single option if \u001b[4moption\u001b[24m is provided) with \u001b[1m-p\u001b[22m, the window options with \u001b[1m-w\u001b[22m, the server options with \u001b[1m-s\u001b[22m, otherwise the session options.  If the option is not a\u001b[m\r\r\n               user option, \u001b[1m-w \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m-s \u001b[22mmay be unnecessary - \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill infer the type from the option name, assuming \u001b[1m-w \u001b[22mfor pane options.  Global session or window options are listed if \u001b[1m-g \u001b[22mis used.   \u001b[1m-v\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               shows  only  the option value, not the name.  If \u001b[1m-q \u001b[22mis set, no error will be returned if \u001b[4moption\u001b[24m is unset.  \u001b[1m-H \u001b[22mincludes hooks (omitted by default).  \u001b[1m-A \u001b[22mincludes options inherited from a\u001b[m\r\r\n               parent set of options, such options are marked with an asterisk.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Available server options are:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mbackspace \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the key sent by \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mfor backspace.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mbuffer-limit \u001b[4m\u001b[22mnumber\u001b[0m\u001b[m"]
[3.302561, "o", "\r\r\n               Set the number of buffers; as new buffers are added to the top of the stack, old ones are removed from the bottom if necessary to maintain this maximum length.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcommand-alias[] \u001b[4m\u001b[22mname=value\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               This is an array of custom aliases for commands.  If an unknown command matches \u001b[4mname\u001b[24m, it is replaced with \u001b[4mvalue\u001b[24m.  For example, after:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mset -s command-alias[100] zoom='resize-pane -Z'\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               Using:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mzoom -t:.1\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               Is equivalent to:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mresize-pane -Z -t:.1\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               Note that aliases are expanded when a command is parsed rather than when it is executed, so binding an alias with \u001b[1mbind-key \u001b[22mwill bind the expanded form.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-command \u001b[4m\u001b[22mshell-command\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Give the command to pipe to if the \u001b[1mcopy-pipe \u001b[22mcopy mode command is used without arguments.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdefault-terminal \u001b[4m\u001b[22mterminal\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the default terminal for new windows created in this session - the default value of the TERM environment variable.  For \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mto work correctly, this \u001b[4mmust\u001b[24m be set to ‘screen’, ‘tmux’\u001b[m\r\r\n               or a derivative of them.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mescape-time \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtime\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the time in milliseconds for which \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwaits after an escape is input to determine if it is part of a function or meta key sequences.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1meditor \u001b[4m\u001b[22mshell-command\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the command used when \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mruns an editor.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mexit-empty \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If enabled (the default), the server will exit when there are no active sessions.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mexit-unattached \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If enabled, the server will exit when there are no attached clients.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mextended-keys \u001b[22m[\u001b["]
[3.303039, "o", "1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1malways\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Controls how modified keys (keys pressed together with Control, Meta, or Shift) are reported.  This is the equivalent of the \u001b[1mmodifyOtherKeys \u001b[4m\u001b[22mxterm\u001b[24m(1) resource.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1373 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.392519, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n               When set to \u001b[1mon\u001b[22m, the program inside the pane can request one of two modes: mode 1 which changes the sequence for only keys which lack an existing well-known representation;  or  mode  2\u001b[m\r\r\n               which changes the sequence for all keys.  When set to \u001b[1malways\u001b[22m, modes 1 and 2 can still be requested by applications, but mode 1 will be forced instead of the standard mode.  When set to\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m, this feature is disabled and only standard keys are reported.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mtmux  \u001b[22mwill  always  request  extended keys itself if the terminal supports them.  See also the \u001b[1mextkeys \u001b[22mfeature for the \u001b[1mterminal-features \u001b[22moption, the \u001b[1mextended-keys-format \u001b[22moption and the\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mpane_key_mode \u001b[22mvariable.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mextended-keys-format \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mcsi-u \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mxterm\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[3.392608, "o", "               Selects one of the two possible formats for reporting modified keys to applications.  This is the equivalent of the \u001b[1mformatOtherKeys \u001b[4m\u001b[22mxterm\u001b[24m(1) resource.  For example, C-S-a will  be  re‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               ported as ‘^[[27;6;65~’ when set to \u001b[1mxterm\u001b[22m, and as ‘^[[65;6u’ when set to \u001b[1mcsi-u\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mfocus-events \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               When  enabled, focus events are requested from the terminal if supported and passed through to applications running in \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[22m.  Attached clients should be detached and attached again af‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               ter changing this option.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mhistory-file \u001b[4m\u001b[22mpath\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               If not empty, a file to which \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill write command prompt history on exit and load it from on start.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmessage-limit \u001b[4m\u001b[22mnumber\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the number of error or information messages to save in the message log for each client.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       "]
[3.393206, "o", "\u001b[1mprompt-history-limit \u001b[4m\u001b[22mnumber\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the number of history items to save in the history file for each type of command prompt.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mset-clipboard \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mexternal \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Attempt to set the terminal clipboard content using the \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1) escape sequence, if there is an \u001b[4mMs\u001b[24m entry in the \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5) description (see the “TERMINFO EXTENSIONS” section).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               If set to \u001b[1mon\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill both accept the escape sequence to create a buffer and attempt to set the terminal clipboard.  If set to \u001b[1mexternal\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill attempt to set the  terminal  clip‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               board but ignore attempts by applications to set \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mbuffers.  If \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill neither accept the clipboard escape sequence nor attempt to set the clipboard.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               Note that this feature needs to be enabled in \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1) by setting the resource:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     disallowedWindowOps: 20,21,SetXprop\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               Or changing this property from the \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1) interactive menu when required.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mterminal-features[] \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set terminal features for terminal types read from \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5).  \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mhas a set of named terminal features.  Each will apply appropriate changes to the \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5) entry in use.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mtmux  \u001b[22mcan detect features for a few common terminals; this option can be used to easily tell tmux about features supported by terminals it cannot detect.  The \u001b[1mterminal-overrides \u001b[22moption\u001b[m\r\r\n               allows individual \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5) capabilities to be set instead, \u001b[1mterminal-features \u001b[22mis intended for classes of functionality supported in a standard way but not  reported  by  \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5).\u001b[m\r\r\n               Care must be taken to configure this only with features the terminal actually supports.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               This  is an array option where each entry is a colon-separated string made up of a terminal type pattern (matched using \u001b[4mfnmatch\u001b[24m(3)) followed by a list of terminal features.  The avail‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               able features are:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               256     Supports 256 colours with the SGR escape sequences.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1422 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.482485, "o", "\r\u001b[K               clipboard\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Allows setting the system clipboard.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               ccolour\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Allows setting the cursor colour.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               cstyle  Allows setting the cursor style.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               extkeys\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Supports extended keys.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               focus   Supports focus reporting.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               hyperlinks\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Supports OSC 8 hyperlinks.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               ignorefkeys\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Ignore function keys from \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5) and use the \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22minternal set only.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               margins\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Supports DECSLRM margins.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               mouse   Supports \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1) mouse sequences.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               osc7    Supports the OSC 7 working directory extension.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               overline\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Supports the overline SGR attribute.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               rectfill\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Supports the DECFRA rec"]
[3.482709, "o", "tangle fill escape sequence.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               RGB     Supports RGB colour with the SGR escape sequences.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               sixel   Supports SIXEL graphics.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               strikethrough\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Supports the strikethrough SGR escape sequence.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               sync    Supports synchronized updates.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               title   Supports \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1) title setting.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               usstyle\u001b[m\r\r\n                       Allows underscore style and colour to be set.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mterminal-overrides[] \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Allow terminal descriptions read using \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5) to be overridden.  Each entry is a colon-separated string made up of a terminal type pattern (matched using \u001b[4mfnmatch\u001b[24m(3)) and a set  of\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[4mname=value\u001b[24m entries.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1471 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.569533, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n               For example, to set the ‘clear’ \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5) entry to ‘\\e[H\\e[2J’ for all terminal types matching ‘rxvt*’:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     \u001b[1mrxvt*:clear=\\e[H\\e[2J\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The terminal entry value is passed through \u001b[4mstrunvis\u001b[24m(3) before interpretation.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1muser-keys[] \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set list of user-defined key escape sequences.  Each item is associated with a key named ‘User0’, ‘User1’, and so on.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               For example:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     set -s user-keys[0] \"\\e[5;30012~\"\u001b[m\r\r\n                     bind User0 resize-pane -L 3\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Available session options are:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mactivity-action \u001b[22m[\u001b[1many \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mnone \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mcurrent \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mother\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set  action on window activity when \u001b[1mmonitor-activity \u001b[22mis on.  \u001b[1many \u001b[22mmeans activity in any window linked to a session causes a bell or message (depending on \u001b[1mvisual-activity\u001b[22m) in t"]
[3.56973, "o", "he current\u001b[m\r\r\n               window of that session, \u001b[1mnone \u001b[22mmeans all activity is ignored (equivalent to \u001b[1mmonitor-activity \u001b[22mbeing off), \u001b[1mcurrent \u001b[22mmeans only activity in windows other than the current window are  ignored\u001b[m\r\r\n               and \u001b[1mother \u001b[22mmeans activity in the current window is ignored but not those in other windows.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1massume-paste-time \u001b[4m\u001b[22mmilliseconds\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               If keys are entered faster than one in \u001b[4mmilliseconds\u001b[24m, they are assumed to have been pasted rather than typed and \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mkey bindings are not processed.  The default is one millisecond and\u001b[m\r\r\n               zero disables.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mbase-index \u001b[4m\u001b[22mindex\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the base index from which an unused index should be searched when a new window is created.  The default is zero.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mbell-action \u001b[22m[\u001b[1many \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mnone \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mcurrent \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mother\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set action on a bell in a window when \u001b[1mmonitor-bell \u001b[22mis on.  The values are the same as those for \u001b[1mactivity-action\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdefault-command \u001b[4m\u001b[22mshell-command\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set  the  command  used for new windows (if not specified when the window is created) to \u001b[4mshell-command\u001b[24m, which may be any \u001b[4msh\u001b[24m(1) command.  The default is an empty string, which instructs\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mto create a login shell using the value of the \u001b[1mdefault-shell \u001b[22moption.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdefault-shell \u001b[4m\u001b[22mpath\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Specify the default shell.  This is used as the login shell for new windows when the \u001b[1mdefault-command \u001b[22moption is set to empty, and must be the full path of the executable.  When  started\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mtmux  \u001b[22mtries to set a default value from the first suitable of the SHELL environment variable, the shell returned by \u001b[4mgetpwuid\u001b[24m(3), or \u001b[4m/bin/sh\u001b[24m.  This option should be configured when \u001b[1mtmux\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               is used as a login shell.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdefault-size \u001b[4m\u001b[22mXxY\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the default size of new windows when the \u001b[1mwindow-size \u001b[22moption is set to manual or when a session is created with \u001b[1mnew-session -d\u001b[22m.  The value is the width and height  separated  by  an\u001b[m\r\r\n               ‘x’ character.  The default is 80x24.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[3.57058, "o", "       \u001b[1mdestroy-unattached \u001b[22m[\u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mkeep-last \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mkeep-group\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If  \u001b[1mon\u001b[22m,  destroy  the session after the last client has detached.  If \u001b[1moff \u001b[22m(the default), leave the session orphaned.  If \u001b[1mkeep-last\u001b[22m, destroy the session only if it is in a group and has\u001b[m\r\r\n               other sessions in that group.  If \u001b[1mkeep-group\u001b[22m, destroy the session unless it is in a group and is the only session in that group.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1520 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.652925, "o", "\r\u001b[K       \u001b[1mdetach-on-destroy \u001b[22m[\u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mno-detached \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mprevious \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mnext\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1mon \u001b[22m(the default), the client is detached when the session it is attached to is destroyed.  If \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m, the client is switched to the most recently active of the remaining sessions.   If\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mno-detached\u001b[22m,  the  client  is detached only if there are no detached sessions; if detached sessions exist, the client is switched to the most recently active.  If \u001b[1mprevious \u001b[22mor \u001b[1mnext\u001b[22m, the\u001b[m\r\r\n               client is switched to the previous or next session in alphabetical order.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdisplay-panes-active-colour \u001b[4m\u001b[22mcolour\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the colour used by the \u001b[1mdisplay-panes \u001b[22mcommand to show the indicator for the active pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdisplay-panes-colour \u001b[4m\u001b[22mcolour\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the colour used by the \u001b[1mdisplay-panes \u001b[22mcommand to show the indicators for inactive pa"]
[3.653681, "o", "nes.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdisplay-panes-time \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtime\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the time in milliseconds for which the indicators shown by the \u001b[1mdisplay-panes \u001b[22mcommand appear.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mdisplay-time \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtime\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the amount of time for which status line messages and other on-screen indicators are displayed.  If set to 0, messages and indicators are displayed until a key is pressed.  \u001b[4mtime\u001b[24m is\u001b[m\r\r\n               in milliseconds.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mhistory-limit \u001b[4m\u001b[22mlines\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the maximum number of lines held in window history.  This setting applies only to new windows - existing window histories are not resized and retain the limit  at  the  point  they\u001b[m\r\r\n               were created.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mkey-table \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey-table\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the default key table to \u001b[4mkey-table\u001b[24m instead of \u001b[4mroot\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlock-after-time \u001b[4m\u001b[22mnumber\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Lock the session (like the \u001b[1mlock-session \u001b[22mcommand) after \u001b[4mnumber\u001b[24m seconds of inactivity.  The default is not to lock (set to 0).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mlock-command \u001b[4m\u001b[22mshell-command\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Command to run when locking each client.  The default is to run \u001b[4mlock\u001b[24m(1) with \u001b[1m-np\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmenu-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the menu style.  See the “STYLES” section on how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m.  Attributes are ignored.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmenu-selected-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the selected menu item style.  See the “STYLES” section on how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m.  Attributes are ignored.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmenu-border-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the menu border style.  See the “STYLES” section on how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m.  Attributes are ignored.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmenu-border-lines \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtype\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the type of characters used for drawing menu borders.  See \u001b[1mpopup-border-lines \u001b[22mfor possible values f"]
[3.653952, "o", "or \u001b[4mborder-lines\u001b[24m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmessage-command-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set status line message command style.  This is used for the command prompt with \u001b[4mvi\u001b[24m(1) keys when in command mode.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmessage-line \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m0 \u001b[22m| \u001b[1m1 \u001b[22m| \u001b[1m2 \u001b[22m| \u001b[1m3 \u001b[22m| \u001b[1m4\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set line on which status line messages and the command prompt are shown.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1569 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.735371, "o", "\r\u001b[K       \u001b[1mmessage-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set status line message style.  This is used for messages and for the command prompt.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmouse \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If on, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mcaptures the mouse and allows mouse events to be bound as key bindings.  See the “MOUSE SUPPORT” section for details.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprefix \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the key accepted as a prefix key.  In addition to the standard keys described under “KEY BINDINGS”, \u001b[1mprefix \u001b[22mcan be set to the special key ‘None’ to set no prefix.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprefix2 \u001b[4m\u001b[22mkey\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set a secondary key accepted as a prefix key.  Like \u001b[1mprefix\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mprefix2 \u001b[22mcan be set to ‘None’.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mprefix-timeout \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtime\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the time in milliseconds for which \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwaits after \u001b[1mprefix \u001b[22mis input before dismissi"]
[3.735682, "o", "ng it.  Can be set to zero to disable any timeout.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrenumber-windows \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If  on,  when  a  window  is  closed in a session, automatically renumber the other windows in numerical order.  This respects the \u001b[1mbase-index \u001b[22moption if it has been set.  If off, do not\u001b[m\r\r\n               renumber the windows.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mrepeat-time \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtime\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Allow multiple commands to be entered without pressing the prefix-key again in the specified \u001b[4mtime\u001b[24m milliseconds (the default is 500).  Whether a key repeats may be set when it is  bound\u001b[m\r\r\n               using the \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mflag to \u001b[1mbind-key\u001b[22m.  Repeat is enabled for the default keys bound to the \u001b[1mresize-pane \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mset-titles \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Attempt to set the client terminal title using the \u001b[4mtsl\u001b[24m and \u001b[4mfsl\u001b[24m \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5) entries if they exist.  \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mautomatically sets t"]
[3.735978, "o", "hese to the \\e]0;...\\007 sequence if the terminal appears to\u001b[m\r\r\n               be \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1).  This option is off by default.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mset-titles-string \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               String used to set the client terminal title if \u001b[1mset-titles \u001b[22mis on.  Formats are expanded, see the “FORMATS” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msilence-action \u001b[22m[\u001b[1many \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mnone \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mcurrent \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mother\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set action on window silence when \u001b[1mmonitor-silence \u001b[22mis on.  The values are the same as those for \u001b[1mactivity-action\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus \u001b[22m[\u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1m2 \u001b[22m| \u001b[1m3 \u001b[22m| \u001b[1m4 \u001b[22m| \u001b[1m5\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Show or hide the status line or specify its size.  Using \u001b[1mon \u001b[22mgives a status line one row in height; \u001b[1m2\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m3\u001b[22m, \u001b[1m4 \u001b[22mor \u001b[1m5 \u001b[22mmore rows.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-format[] \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Specify the format to be used for each line of the status line.  The default builds the top status line from the various individual status options below.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-interval \u001b[4m\u001b[22minterval\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Update the status line every \u001b[4minterval\u001b[24m seconds.  By default, updates will occur every 15 seconds.  A setting of zero disables redrawing at interval.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-justify \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mleft \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mcentre \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mright \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mabsolute-centre\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the position of the window list in the status line: left, centre or right.  centre puts the window list in the relative centre of the available free space; absolute-centre uses the\u001b[m\r\r\n               centre of the entire horizontal space.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-keys \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mvi \u001b[22m| \u001b[1memacs\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Use  vi  or emacs-style key bindings in the status line, for example at the command prompt.  The default is emacs, unless the VISUAL or EDITOR environment variables are set and contain\u001b[m\r\r\n               the string ‘vi’.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1618 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.823852, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-left \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Display \u001b[4mstring\u001b[24m (by default the session name) to the left of the status line.  \u001b[4mstring\u001b[24m will be passed through \u001b[4mstrftime\u001b[24m(3).  Also see the “FORMATS” and “STYLES” sections.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               For details on how the names and titles can be set see the “NAMES AND TITLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               Examples are:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[3.824427, "o", "                     #(sysctl vm.loadavg)\u001b[m\r\r\n                     #[fg=yellow,bold]#(apm -l)%%#[default] [#S]\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               The default is ‘[#S] ’.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-left-length \u001b[4m\u001b[22mlength\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the maximum \u001b[4mlength\u001b[24m of the left component of the status line.  The default is 10.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-left-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the style of the left part of the status line.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-position \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mtop \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mbottom\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the position of the status line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-right \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Display \u001b[4mstring\u001b[24m to the right of the status line.  By default, the current pane title in double quotes, the date and the time are shown.  As with \u001b[1mstatus-left\u001b[22m, \u001b[4mstring\u001b[24m will  be  passed  to\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[4mstrftime\u001b[24m(3) and character pairs are replaced.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-right-length \u001b[4m\u001b[22mlength\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the maximum \u001b[4mlength\u001b[24m of the right component of the status line.  The default is 40.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-right-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the style of the right part of the status line.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mstatus-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set status line style.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mupdate-environment[] \u001b[4m\u001b[22mvariable\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set  list  of  environment variables to be copied into the session environment when a new session is created or an existing session is attached.  Any variables that do not exist in the\u001b[m\r\r\n               source environment are set to be removed from the session environment (as if \u001b[1m-r \u001b[22mwas given to the \u001b[1mset-environment \u001b[22mcommand).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mvisual-activity \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mboth\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If on, display a message instead of sending a bell when activity occurs in a window for which the \u001b[1mmonitor-activity \u001b[22mwindow option is enabled.  If set to both, a bell and a  message  are\u001b[m\r\r\n               produced.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mvisual-bell \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mboth\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If  on,  a message is shown on a bell in a window for which the \u001b[1mmonitor-bell \u001b[22mwindow option is enabled instead of it being passed through to the terminal (which normally makes a sound).\u001b[m\r\r\n               If set to both, a bell and a message are produced.  Also see the \u001b[1mbell-action \u001b[22moption.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mvisual-silence \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mboth\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If \u001b[1mmonitor-silence \u001b[22mis enabled, prints a message after the interval has expired on a given window instead of sending a bell.  If set to both, a bell and a message are produced.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1667 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[3.914164, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mword-separators \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Sets the session's conception of what characters are considered word separators, for the purposes of the next and previous word commands in copy mode.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Available window options are:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1maggressive-resize \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Aggressively resize the chosen window.  This means that \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill resize the window to the size of the smallest or largest session (see the \u001b[1mwindow-size \u001b[22moption) for which it is the cur‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               rent window, rather than the session to which it is attached.  The window may resize when the current window is changed on another session; this option is good for full-screen programs\u001b[m\r\r\n               which support SIGWINCH and poor for interactive programs such as shells.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mautomatic-rename \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Control automatic window renaming.  When this setting is enabled, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mwill rename the window automatically using the format specified by \u001b[1mautomatic-rename-format\u001b[22m.  This flag  is  auto‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               matically  disabled  for  an individual window when a name is specified at creation with \u001b[1mnew-window \u001b[22mor \u001b[1mnew-session\u001b[22m, or later with \u001b[1mrename-window\u001b[22m, or with a terminal escape sequence.  It\u001b[m\r\r\n               may be switched off globally with:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n                     set-option -wg automatic-rename off\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mautomatic-rename-format \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               The format (see “FORMATS”) used when the \u001b[1mautomatic-rename \u001b[22moption is enabled.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mclock-mode-colour \u001b[4m\u001b[22mcolour\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set clock colour.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mclock-mode-style \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m12 \u001b[22m| \u001b[1m24\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set clock hour format.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mfill-character \u001b[4m\u001b[22mcharacter\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the character used to fill areas of the terminal unused by a window.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmain-pane-height \u001b[4"]
[3.914411, "o", "m\u001b[22mheight\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmain-pane-width \u001b[4m\u001b[22mwidth\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the width or height of the main (left or top) pane in the \u001b[1mmain-horizontal\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mmain-horizontal-mirrored\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mmain-vertical\u001b[22m, or \u001b[1mmain-vertical-mirrored \u001b[22mlayouts.  If suffixed by ‘%’, this  is\u001b[m\r\r\n               a percentage of the window size.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-mode-match-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the style of search matches in copy mode.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-mode-mark-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the style of the line containing the mark in copy mode.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcopy-mode-current-match-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the style of the current search match in copy mode.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmode-keys \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mvi \u001b[22m| \u001b[1memacs\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Use vi or emacs-style key bindings in copy mode.  The default is emacs, unless VISUAL or EDITOR contains ‘vi’.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmode-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set window modes style.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1716 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[4.003475, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[4.00355, "o", "\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmonitor-activity \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Monitor for activity in the window.  Windows with activity are highlighted in the status line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmonitor-bell \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Monitor for a bell in the window.  Windows with a bell are highlighted in the status line.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mmonitor-silence \u001b[22m[\u001b[1minterval\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Monitor  for  silence (no activity) in the window within \u001b[1minterval \u001b[22mseconds.  Windows that have been silent for the interval are highlighted in the status line.  An interval of zero dis‐\u001b[m\r\r\n               ables the monitoring.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mother-pane-height \u001b[4m\u001b[22mheight\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the height of the other panes (not the main pane) in the \u001b[1mmain-horizontal \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mmain-horizontal-mirrored \u001b[22mlayouts.  If this option is set to 0 (the default), it will  have  no  effect.\u001b[m\r\r\n               If  both  the  \u001b[1mmain-pane-height  \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mother-pane-h"]
[4.004059, "o", "eight \u001b[22moptions are set, the main pane will grow taller to make the other panes the specified height, but will never shrink to do so.  If\u001b[m\r\r\n               suffixed by ‘%’, this is a percentage of the window size.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mother-pane-width \u001b[4m\u001b[22mwidth\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Like \u001b[1mother-pane-height\u001b[22m, but set the width of other panes in the \u001b[1mmain-vertical \u001b[22mand \u001b[1mmain-vertical-mirrored \u001b[22mlayouts.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpane-active-border-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the pane border style for the currently active pane.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.  Attributes are ignored.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpane-base-index \u001b[4m\u001b[22mindex\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Like \u001b[1mbase-index\u001b[22m, but set the starting index for pane numbers.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpane-border-format \u001b[4m\u001b[22mformat\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the text shown in pane border status lines.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpane-border-indicators \u001b[22m[\u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mcolour \u001b[22m| \u001b[1marrows \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mboth\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Indicate active pane by colouring only half of the border in windows with exactly two panes, by displaying arrow markers, by drawing both or neither.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpane-border-lines \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtype\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the type of characters used for drawing pane borders.  \u001b[4mtype\u001b[24m may be one of:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               single  single lines using ACS or UTF-8 characters\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               double  double lines using UTF-8 characters\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               heavy   heavy lines using UTF-8 characters\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               simple  simple ASCII characters\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               number  the pane number\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               ‘double’ and ‘heavy’ will fall back to standard ACS line drawing when UTF-8 is not supported.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpane-border-status \u001b[22m[\u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mtop \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mbottom\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Turn pane border status lines off or set their position.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1765 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[4.088366, "o", "\r\u001b[K       \u001b[1mpane-border-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the pane border style for panes aside from the active pane.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.  Attributes are ignored.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpopup-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the popup style.  See the “STYLES” section on how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m.  Attributes are ignored.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpopup-border-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the popup border style.  See the “STYLES” section on how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m.  Attributes are ignored.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpopup-border-lines \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtype\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the type of characters used for drawing popup borders.  \u001b[4mtype\u001b[24m may be one of:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               single  single lines using ACS or UTF-8 characters (default)\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               rounded\u001b[m\r\r\n                       variation of single with rounded corners using UTF-8 characters\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               double  double lines using UTF-8 characters\u001b[m\r\r\n"]
[4.088699, "o", "\u001b[m\r\r\n               heavy   heavy lines using UTF-8 characters\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               simple  simple ASCII characters\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               padded  simple ASCII space character\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               none    no border\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               ‘double’ and ‘heavy’ will fall back to standard ACS line drawing when UTF-8 is not supported.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-status-activity-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set status line style for windows with an activity alert.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-status-bell-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set status line style for windows with a bell alert.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-status-current-format \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Like \u001b[4mwindow-status-format\u001b[24m, but is the format used when the window is the current window.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-status-current-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set status "]
[4.089589, "o", "line style for the currently active window.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-status-format \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the format in which the window is displayed in the status line window list.  See the “FORMATS” and “STYLES” sections.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-status-last-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set status line style for the last active window.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-status-separator \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Sets the separator drawn between windows in the status line.  The default is a single space character.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1814 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[4.178323, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[4.178807, "o", "\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-status-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set status line style for a single window.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-size \u001b[4m\u001b[22mlargest\u001b[24m | \u001b[4msmallest\u001b[24m | \u001b[4mmanual\u001b[24m | \u001b[4mlatest\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Configure how \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mdetermines the window size.  If set to \u001b[4mlargest\u001b[24m, the size of the largest attached session is used; if \u001b[4msmallest\u001b[24m, the size of the smallest.  If \u001b[4mmanual\u001b[24m, the  size  of  a\u001b[m\r\r\n               new  window  is  set from the \u001b[1mdefault-size \u001b[22moption and windows are resized automatically.  With \u001b[4mlatest\u001b[24m, \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22muses the size of the client that had the most recent activity.  See also the\u001b[m\r\r\n               \u001b[1mresize-window \u001b[22mcommand and the \u001b[1maggressive-resize \u001b[22moption.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwrap-search \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               If this option is set, searches will wrap around the end of the pane contents.  The default is on.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Available pane options are:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mallow-passthrough \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mall\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Allow programs in the pane to bypass \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22musing a terminal escape sequence (\\ePtmux;...\\e\\\\).  If set to \u001b[1mon\u001b[22m, passthrough sequences will be allowed only if the pane is visible.  If  set\u001b[m\r\r\n               to \u001b[1mall\u001b[22m, they will be allowed even if the pane is invisible.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mallow-rename \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Allow programs in the pane to change the window name using a terminal escape sequence (\\ek...\\e\\\\).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mallow-set-title \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Allow programs in the pane to change the title using the terminal escape sequences (\\e]2;...\\e\\\\ or \\e]0;...\\e\\\\).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1malternate-screen \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               This option configures whether programs running inside the pane may use the terminal alternate screen feature, which allows the \u001b[4msmcup\u001b[24m and \u001b[4mrmcup\u001b[24m \u001b[4mterminfo\u001b[24m(5) capabilities.  The alternate\u001b[m\r\r\n               screen  feature  preserves the contents of the window when an interactive application starts and restores it on exit, so that any output visible before the application starts reappears\u001b[m\r\r\n               unchanged after it exits.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcursor-colour \u001b[4m\u001b[22mcolour\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the colour of the cursor.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mpane-colours[] \u001b[4m\u001b[22mcolour\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               The default colour palette.  Each entry in the array defines the colour \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22muses when the colour with that index is requested.  The index may be from zero to 255.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mcursor-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the style of the cursor.  Available styles are: \u001b[1mdefault\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mblinking-block\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mblock\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mblinking-underline\u001b[22m, \u001b[1munderline\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mblinking-bar\u001b[22m, \u001b[1mbar\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mremain-on-exit \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff \u001b[22m| \u001b[1mfailed\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               A pane with this flag set is not destroyed when the program running in it exits.  If set to \u001b[1mfailed\u001b[22m, then only when the program exit status is not zero.  The  pane  may  be  reactivated\u001b[m\r\r\n               with the \u001b[1mrespawn-pane \u001b[22mcommand.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mremain-on-exit-format \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstring\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the text shown at the bottom of exited panes when \u001b[1mremain-on-exit \u001b[22mis enabled.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mscroll-on-clear \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               When the entire screen is cleared and this option is on, scroll the contents of the screen into history before clearing it.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1msynchronize-panes \u001b[22m[\u001b[1mon \u001b[22m| \u001b[1moff\u001b[22m]\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1863 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[4.261676, "o", "\r\u001b[K"]
[4.261899, "o", "               Duplicate input to all other panes in the same window where this option is also on (only for panes that are not in any mode).\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-active-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the pane style when it is the active pane.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mwindow-style \u001b[4m\u001b[22mstyle\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Set the pane style.  For how to specify \u001b[4mstyle\u001b[24m, see the “STYLES” section.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mHOOKS\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mallows commands to run on various triggers, called \u001b[4mhooks\u001b[24m.  Most \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mcommands have an \u001b[4mafter\u001b[24m hook and there are a number of hooks not associated with commands.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Hooks  are  stored  as array options, members of the array are executed in order when the hook is triggered.  Like options different hooks may be global or belong to a session, window or pane.\u001b[m\r\r\n       Hooks may be configured with the \u001b[1mset-hook \u001b[22mor \u001b[1mset-option \u001b[22mcommands and displayed with \u001b[1mshow-hooks \u001b[22mor \u001b[1mshow-options -H\u001b[22m.  The following two commands are equivalent:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n              set-hook -g pane-mode-changed[42] 'set -g status-left-style bg=red'\u001b[m\r\r\n              set-option -g pane-mode-changed[42] 'set -g status-left-style bg=red'\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Setting a hook without specifying an array index clears the hook and sets the first member of the array.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       A command's after hook is run after it completes, except when the command is run as part of a hook itself.  They are named with an ‘after-’ prefix.  For example, the following command  adds  a\u001b[m\r\r\n       hook to select the even-vertical layout after every \u001b[1msplit-window\u001b[22m:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             set-hook -g after-split-window \"selectl even-vertical\"\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       If a command fails, the ‘command-error’ hook will be fired.  For example, this could be used to write to a log file:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             set-hook -g command-error \"run-shell \\\"echo 'a tmux command failed' >>/tmp/log\\\"\"\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       All the notifications listed in the “CONTROL MODE” section are hooks (without any arguments), except \u001b[1m%exit\u001b[22m.  The following additional hooks are available:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       alert-activity          Run when a window has activity.  See \u001b[1mmonitor-activity\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       alert-bell              Run when a window has received a bell.  See \u001b[1mmonitor-bell\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       alert-silence           Run when a window has been silent.  See \u001b[1mmonitor-silence\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       client-active           Run when a client becomes the latest active client of its session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       client-attached         Run when a client is attached.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       client-detached         Run when a client is detached\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       client-focus-in         Run when focus enters a client\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       client-focus-out        Run when focus exits a client\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       client-resized          Run when a client is resized.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       client-session-changed  Run when a client's attached session is changed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1912 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[4.352435, "o", "\r\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n       command-error           Run when a command fails.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       pane-died               Run when the program running in a pane exits, but \u001b[1mremain-on-exit \u001b[22mis on so the pane has not closed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       pane-exited             Run when the program running in a pane exits.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       pane-focus-in           Run when the focus enters a pane, if the \u001b[1mfocus-events \u001b[22moption is on.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       pane-focus-out          Run when the focus exits a pane, if the \u001b[1mfocus-events \u001b[22moption is on.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       pane-set-clipboard      Run when the terminal clipboard is set using the \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1) escape sequence.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       session-created         Run when a new session created.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       session-closed          Run when a session closed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       session-renamed         Run when a session is renamed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       window-linked           Run when a window is linked into a session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       window-renamed          Run when a window is renamed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       window-resized          Run when a window is resized.  This may be after the \u001b[4mclient-resized\u001b[24m hook is run.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       window-unlinked         Run when a window is unlinked from a session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       Hooks are managed with these commands:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mset-hook \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-agpRuw\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mhook-name\u001b[24m \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n               Without \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m, sets (or with \u001b[1m-u \u001b[22munsets) hook \u001b[4mhook-name\u001b[24m to \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m.  The flags are the same as for \u001b[1mset-option\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n               With \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m, run \u001b[4mhook-name\u001b[24m immediately.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       \u001b[1mshow-hooks \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-gpw\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n               Shows hooks.  The flags are the same as for \u001b[1mshow-options\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1mMOUSE SUPPORT\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n       If  the \u001b[1mmouse \u001b[22moption is on (the default is off), \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mallows mouse events to be bound as keys.  The name of each key is made up of a mouse event (such as ‘MouseUp1’) and a location suffix, one\u001b[m\r\r\n       of the following:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1mPane             \u001b[22mthe contents of a pane\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1mBorder           \u001b[22ma pane border\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1mStatus           \u001b[22mthe status line window list\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1mStatusLeft       \u001b[22mthe left part of the status line\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1mStatusRight      \u001b[22mthe right part of the status line\u001b[m\r\r\n             \u001b[1mStatusDefault    \u001b[22many other part of the status line\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[m\r\r\n       The following mouse events are available:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7m Manual page tmux(1) line 1961 (press h for help or q to quit)\u001b[27m\u001b[K"]
[4.439806, "o", "\r\u001b[K/\u001b[KO\bO\u001b[KP\bP\u001b[KT\bT\u001b[KI\bI\u001b[KO\bO\u001b[KN\bN\u001b[KS\bS\r\u001b[K"]
[4.440493, "o", "\u001b[1;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[2;1H\u001b[K       command-error           Run when a command fails.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[3;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[4;1H\u001b[K       pane-died               Run when the program running in a pane exits, but \u001b[1mremain-on-exit \u001b[22mis on so the pane has not closed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[5;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[6;1H\u001b[K       pane-exited             Run when the program running in a pane exits.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[8;1H\u001b[K       pane-focus-in           Run when the focus enters a pane, if the \u001b[1mfocus-events \u001b[22moption is on.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[9;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[10;1H\u001b[K       pane-focus-out          Run when the focus exits a pane, if the \u001b[1mfocus-events \u001b[22moption is on.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[11;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[12;1H\u001b[K       pane-set-clipboard      Run when the terminal clipboard is set using the \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1) escape sequence.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[13;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[14;1H\u001b[K       session-created         Run when a new session created.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[15;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[16;1H\u001b[K       session-closed          Run when a session closed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[17;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[18;1H\u001b[K       session-renamed         Run when a session is renamed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[1"]
[4.441025, "o", "9;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[20;1H\u001b[K       window-linked           Run when a window is linked into a session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[21;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[22;1H\u001b[K       window-renamed          Run when a window is renamed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[23;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[24;1H\u001b[K       window-resized          Run when a window is resized.  This may be after the \u001b[4mclient-resized\u001b[24m hook is run.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[25;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[26;1H\u001b[K       window-unlinked         Run when a window is unlinked from a session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[27;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[28;1H\u001b[K       Hooks are managed with these commands:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[29;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[30;1H\u001b[K       \u001b[1mset-hook \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-agpRuw\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mhook-name\u001b[24m \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[31;1H\u001b[K               Without \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m, sets (or with \u001b[1m-u \u001b[22munsets) hook \u001b[4mhook-name\u001b[24m to \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m.  The flags are the same as for \u001b[1mset-option\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[32;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[33;1H\u001b[K               With \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m, run \u001b[4mhook-name\u001b[24m immediately.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[34;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[35;1H\u001b[K       \u001b[1mshow-hooks \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-gpw\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pan"]
[4.441191, "o", "e\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[36;1H\u001b[K               Shows hooks.  The flags are the same as for \u001b[1mshow-options\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[37;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[38;1H\u001b[K\u001b[1mMOUSE SUPPORT\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[39;1H\u001b[K       If  the \u001b[1mmouse \u001b[22moption is on (the default is off), \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mallows mouse events to be bound as keys.  The name of each key is made up of a mouse event (such as ‘MouseUp1’) and a location suffix, one\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[40;1H\u001b[K       of the following:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[41;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[42;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mPane             \u001b[22mthe contents of a pane\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[43;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mBorder           \u001b[22ma pane border\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[44;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mStatus           \u001b[22mthe status line window list\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[45;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mStatusLeft       \u001b[22mthe left part of the status line\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[46;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mStatusRight      \u001b[22mthe right part of the status line\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[47;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mStatusDefault    \u001b[22many other part of the status line\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[48;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[49;1H\u001b[K       The following mouse events are available:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[50;1H\u001b[1;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[2;1H\u001b"]
[4.441624, "o", "[K       command-error           Run when a command fails.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[3;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[4;1H\u001b[K       pane-died               Run when the program running in a pane exits, but \u001b[1mremain-on-exit \u001b[22mis on so the pane has not closed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[5;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[6;1H\u001b[K       pane-exited             Run when the program running in a pane exits.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[7;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[8;1H\u001b[K       pane-focus-in           Run when the focus enters a pane, if the \u001b[1mfocus-events \u001b[22moption is on.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[9;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[10;1H\u001b[K       pane-focus-out          Run when the focus exits a pane, if the \u001b[1mfocus-events \u001b[22moption is on.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[11;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[12;1H\u001b[K       pane-set-clipboard      Run when the terminal clipboard is set using the \u001b[4mxterm\u001b[24m(1) escape sequence.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[13;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[14;1H\u001b[K       session-created         Run when a new session created.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[15;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[16;1H\u001b[K       session-closed          Run when a session closed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[17;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[18;1H\u001b[K       session-renamed         Run when a session is renamed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[19;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[20;1H\u001b["]
[4.441819, "o", "K       window-linked           Run when a window is linked into a session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[21;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[22;1H\u001b[K       window-renamed          Run when a window is renamed.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[23;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[24;1H\u001b[K       window-resized          Run when a window is resized.  This may be after the \u001b[4mclient-resized\u001b[24m hook is run.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[25;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[26;1H\u001b[K       window-unlinked         Run when a window is unlinked from a session.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[27;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[28;1H\u001b[K       Hooks are managed with these commands:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[29;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[30;1H\u001b[K       \u001b[1mset-hook \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-agpRuw\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m] \u001b[4mhook-name\u001b[24m \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[31;1H\u001b[K               Without \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m, sets (or with \u001b[1m-u \u001b[22munsets) hook \u001b[4mhook-name\u001b[24m to \u001b[4mcommand\u001b[24m.  The flags are the same as for \u001b[1mset-option\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[32;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[33;1H\u001b[K               With \u001b[1m-R\u001b[22m, run \u001b[4mhook-name\u001b[24m immediately.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[34;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[35;1H\u001b[K       \u001b[1mshow-hooks \u001b[22m[\u001b[1m-gpw\u001b[22m] [\u001b[1m-t \u001b[4m\u001b[22mtarget-pane\u001b[24m]\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[36;1H\u001b["]
[4.442261, "o", "K               Shows hooks.  The flags are the same as for \u001b[1mshow-options\u001b[22m.\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[37;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[38;1H\u001b[K\u001b[1mMOUSE SUPPORT\u001b[0m\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[39;1H\u001b[K       If  the \u001b[1mmouse \u001b[22moption is on (the default is off), \u001b[1mtmux \u001b[22mallows mouse events to be bound as keys.  The name of each key is made up of a mouse event (such as ‘MouseUp1’) and a location suffix, one\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[40;1H\u001b[K       of the following:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[41;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[42;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mPane             \u001b[22mthe contents of a pane\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[43;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mBorder           \u001b[22ma pane border\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[44;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mStatus           \u001b[22mthe status line window list\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[45;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mStatusLeft       \u001b[22mthe left part of the status line\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[46;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mStatusRight      \u001b[22mthe right part of the status line\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[47;1H\u001b[K             \u001b[1mStatusDefault    \u001b[22many other part of the status line\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[48;1H\u001b[K\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[49;1H\u001b[K       The following mouse events are available:\u001b[m\r\r\n\u001b[50;1H"]
